/**
 * Teach Me explanations for mathematics, written in advance so they load
 * instantly, work offline and cost nothing per student. Keyed by
 * passageId|question|options (see teachMeKey in app.js). A question with no
 * entry here falls back to the live AI explanation.
 */
(window.TEACH_ME = window.TEACH_ME || {})["mathematics"] = {
 "|Simplify: (√3 + √5)(√3 − √5)|−2|2|−8|8": "Step 1: Spot the pattern (a + b)(a − b) = a^{2} − b^{2}. Step 2: (√3)^{2} − (√5)^{2} = 3 − 5 = −2: A. Trap: B forgets that the result is 3 − 5, not 5 − 3; C and D come from squaring 3 and 5 instead of their roots. Memory tip: (√a)^{2} = a, so \"difference of two squares\" removes surds instantly.",
 "|If 2^(x+1) = 32, find x.|3|4|5|6": "Step 1: Write 32 as a power of 2: 32 = 2^{5}. Step 2: Equate the powers: x + 1 = 5. Step 3: x = 4: B. Trap: C (5) stops at the power of 32 and forgets to subtract the 1. Memory tip: same base on both sides → equate the powers.",
 "|Factorize completely: 6x² + 7x − 3|(3x − 1)(2x + 3)|(6x − 1)(x + 3)|(3x + 1)(2x − 3)|(2x + 3)(3x − 1)": "Step 1: For 6x^{2} + 7x − 3, find two numbers with product 6 × (−3) = −18 and sum 7: 9 and −2. Step 2: 6x^{2} + 9x − 2x − 3 = 3x(2x + 3) − 1(2x + 3) = (3x − 1)(2x + 3): A. D is the same two factors in the other order, so it is also accepted. Check by expanding: 6x^{2} + 9x − 2x − 3 ✓. Memory tip: always expand your factors to check; the order of the brackets doesn't matter.",
 "|If the mean of 3, 7, x, 12, and 15 is 9, find x.|6|7|8|9": "Step 1: Mean × number of values = total: 9 × 5 = 45. Step 2: The known values add to 3 + 7 + 12 + 15 = 37. Step 3: x = 45 − 37 = 8: C. Memory tip: for a missing value, work with totals, not averages.",
 "|Find the area of a circle with circumference 44 cm. (Take π = 22/7)|77 cm²|154 cm²|308 cm²|616 cm²": "Step 1: C = 2πr → 44 = 2 × 22/7 × r → r = 7 cm. Step 2: Area = πr^{2} = 22/7 × 49 = 154 cm²: B. Trap: A (77) halves the area by mistake; D (616) uses the diameter as the radius. Memory tip: find r first, and remember r is half the diameter.",
 "|In the diagram, the interior angles of a triangle are 2x°, 3x° and 4x°. Find x.|10°|20°|30°|40°": "Step 1: Angles in a triangle add to 180°: 2x + 3x + 4x = 180. Step 2: 9x = 180, so x = 20°: B. Check: 40° + 60° + 80° = 180° ✓. Memory tip: add the coefficients (2 + 3 + 4 = 9) and divide 180 by them.",
 "|Evaluate: ³√(−27) + ⁴√16|−1|1|5|−5": "Step 1: ∛(−27) = −3, because (−3)^{3} = −27. Step 2: ⁴√16 = 2, because 2^{4} = 16. Step 3: −3 + 2 = −1: A. Trap: C (5) ignores the minus sign; an odd root of a negative number is negative. Memory tip: odd roots keep the sign; even roots of negatives are not real.",
 "|If P = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5} and Q = {2, 4, 6, 8}, find P ∪ Q.|{1,2,3,4,5,6,8}|{2,4}|{1,3,5}|{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8}": "The union P ∪ Q lists every element that is in P or Q (or both), written once: {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8}: A. B is the intersection (in both), C is \"P only\", and D wrongly adds 7, which is in neither set. Memory tip: ∪ = \"or\" (everything), ∩ = \"and\" (common elements).",
 "|A fair die is thrown once. Find the probability of getting a prime number.|1/6|1/3|1/2|2/3": "The primes on a die are 2, 3 and 5 (1 is not prime). P = 3/6 = 1/2: C. Trap: B (1/3) misses one prime; D (2/3) wrongly counts 1 as prime. Memory tip: 1 is not a prime number; 2 is the only even prime.",
 "|Find the gradient of the line joining (−2, 3) and (4, −9).|2|−2|3|−3": "Gradient = (y_{2} − y_{1})/(x_{2} − x_{1}) = (−9 − 3)/(4 − (−2)) = −12/6 = −2: B. Trap: A drops the negative sign; the line falls from left to right, so the gradient must be negative. Memory tip: subtract in the same order on top and bottom.",
 "|Simplify: (2x²y³)³ ÷ (4x³y²)|2x³y⁷|2x³y⁵|x³y⁷|2xy⁷": "Step 1: (2x^{2}y^{3})^{3} = 8x^{6}y^{9} (cube the 2, multiply the powers by 3). Step 2: Divide by 4x^{3}y^{2}: 8/4 = 2, x^{6−3} = x^{3}, y^{9−2} = y^{7}. Answer 2x^{3}y^{7}: A. Trap: forgetting to cube the 2 gives C. Memory tip: a power outside a bracket applies to the number too.",
 "|The bar chart shows the sales of oranges over 4 days. If Monday = 30, Tuesday = 50, Wednesday = 40, Thursday = 20, find the mean daily sales.|30|35|40|45": "Mean = total ÷ number of days = (30 + 50 + 40 + 20)/4 = 140/4 = 35: B. Trap: C (40) is just one day's value, and A (30) is Monday. Memory tip: read every bar carefully, add them, then divide by how many bars there are.",
 "|If y = 3x² − 2x + 5, find dy/dx.|6x − 2|3x − 2|6x + 5|x² − 2": "Differentiate term by term with d/dx(x^{n}) = nx^{n−1}: 3x^{2} → 6x, −2x → −2, and 5 → 0. So dy/dx = 6x − 2: A. Trap: C keeps the constant 5; constants differentiate to zero. Memory tip: \"bring the power down, reduce it by one, and constants vanish\".",
 "|Solve: |2x − 3| = 7|x = 5 or x = −2|x = 5 or x = 2|x = −5 or x = 2|x = 5 only": "|2x − 3| = 7 means 2x − 3 = 7 or 2x − 3 = −7. First: 2x = 10, x = 5. Second: 2x = −4, x = −2. Answer A. Trap: D forgets the negative case. Memory tip: an absolute value equation |…| = k always gives two equations, … = k and … = −k.",
 "|A man travels 10 km due north and then 24 km due east. Find the shortest distance from his starting point.|26 km|34 km|14 km|22 km": "North and east are at right angles, so use Pythagoras: √(10^{2} + 24^{2}) = √(100 + 576) = √676 = 26 km: A. Trap: B (34) just adds the two distances. Memory tip: 10-24-26 is the 5-12-13 triple doubled.",
 "|Find the angle subtended at the centre of a circle of radius 6 cm by an arc of length 3π cm.|30°|45°|90°|60°": "Arc length = rθ with θ in radians: 3π = 6θ → θ = π/2 radians = 90°: C. Or use degrees: arc = (θ/360) × 2πr → 3π = (θ/360) × 12π → θ = 90°. Memory tip: π radians = 180°.",
 "|If the roots of 2x² + kx + 3 = 0 are equal, find k.|2√6|4√6|√6|3√6": "Equal roots means the discriminant is zero: b^{2} − 4ac = 0 → k^{2} − 4(2)(3) = 0 → k^{2} = 24 → k = ±√24 = ±2√6. The option given is 2√6: A. Trap: B (4√6) is double the correct value. Memory tip: equal roots ↔ b^{2} = 4ac.",
 "|Evaluate: ∫₀² (3x² − 2x) dx|4|6|8|12": "Integrate: ∫(3x^{2} − 2x) dx = x^{3} − x^{2}. Evaluate from 0 to 2: (8 − 4) − (0 − 0) = 4: A. Trap: C (8) forgets to subtract the x^{2} term. Memory tip: integrate term by term, then \"top limit minus bottom limit\".",
 "|A student's score in six tests are: 60, 72, 54, 80, 66, 68. Find the median.|67|68|69|70": "Step 1: Arrange in order: 54, 60, 66, 68, 72, 80. Step 2: There are 6 values, so the median is the average of the 3rd and 4th: (66 + 68)/2 = 67: A. Trap: B (68) just takes the 4th value. Memory tip: for an even count, average the two middle values.",
 "|From a point 150 m from the base of a tower, the angle of elevation of the top is 30°. Find the height of the tower.|50√3 m|75 m|150√3 m|50 m": "tan 30° = height/150, so height = 150 × tan 30° = 150 × 1/√3 = 150√3/3 = 50√3 m: A. Trap: C (150√3) uses tan 60° instead of tan 30°. Memory tip: tan 30° = 1/√3 and tan 60° = √3; elevation questions use tan = opposite/adjacent.",
 "|How many ways can the letters of the word 'GREAT' be arranged?|24|60|120|240": "GREAT has 5 different letters, so they can be arranged in 5! = 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 120 ways: C. Trap: A (24) is 4!, which leaves out one letter. Memory tip: n different objects can be arranged in n! ways; divide only when letters repeat.",
 "|Find the value of x if: (1/3)^x = 27|3|−3|9|−9": "Write both sides as powers of 3: (1/3)^{x} = 3^{−x} and 27 = 3^{3}. So −x = 3 and x = −3: B. Trap: A (3) forgets that 1/3 = 3^{−1}. Memory tip: a fraction like 1/a is a^{−1}.",
 "|If sin θ = 3/5 and θ is acute, find tan θ.|3/4|4/3|3/5|5/3": "sin θ = 3/5 gives a 3-4-5 right triangle: opposite = 3, hypotenuse = 5, so adjacent = 4. tan θ = opposite/adjacent = 3/4: A. Trap: B (4/3) turns the ratio upside down. Memory tip: SOH CAH TOA, and use the 3-4-5 triple.",
 "|Simplify: log 8 + log 5 − log 2 (using base 10)|log 20|log 15|log 40|log 10": "Use the log laws: log 8 + log 5 = log 40, and log 40 − log 2 = log 20. Answer log 20: A. Trap: C (log 40) forgets the subtraction. Memory tip: + log → multiply, − log → divide.",
 "|The graph of y = x² − 4 cuts the x-axis at:|x = ±2|x = 4 only|x = ±4|x = 2 only": "The graph cuts the x-axis where y = 0: x^{2} − 4 = 0 → x^{2} = 4 → x = ±2: A. Trap: D forgets the negative root. Memory tip: x^{2} = k gives two roots, ±√k.",
 "|The pie chart shows how a family spends its monthly income. If the total income is ₦120,000 and the rent sector is 90°, how much is spent on rent?|₦30,000|₦40,000|₦45,000|₦60,000": "A pie chart's 360° stands for the whole income. Rent = 90/360 = 1/4 of ₦120,000 = ₦30,000: A. Trap: B (₦40,000) uses 1/3 instead of 1/4. Memory tip: amount = (sector angle ÷ 360) × total.",
 "|Find the sum to infinity of the GP: 1 + 1/3 + 1/9 + ...|3/2|2/3|1/2|3": "This is a GP with a = 1 and r = 1/3. Since −1 < r < 1, S_{∞} = a/(1 − r) = 1/(2/3) = 3/2: A. Trap: B (2/3) inverts the answer. Memory tip: the sum to infinity exists only when the ratio is between −1 and 1.",
 "|If the quadratic equation x² − 5x + k = 0 has one root as 3, find k.|6|8|12|15": "If 3 is a root, it satisfies the equation: 3^{2} − 5(3) + k = 0 → 9 − 15 + k = 0 → k = 6: A. Check: x^{2} − 5x + 6 = (x − 2)(x − 3) ✓. Memory tip: a root makes the equation true, so substitute it.",
 "|Find the remainder when x³ − 5x² + 7x − 2 is divided by (x − 2).|0|2|4|6": "By the remainder theorem, the remainder on dividing by (x − 2) is f(2) = 8 − 20 + 14 − 2 = 0: A. So (x − 2) is in fact a factor. Trap: substituting x = −2 instead of x = 2. Memory tip: dividing by (x − a) → substitute x = a.",
 "|Solve simultaneously: 2x + 3y = 12 and x − y = 1|x=3, y=2|x=2, y=3|x=4, y=1|x=1, y=4": "From x − y = 1, x = y + 1. Substitute: 2(y + 1) + 3y = 12 → 5y = 10 → y = 2, so x = 3: A. Check in both equations: 6 + 6 = 12 ✓ and 3 − 2 = 1 ✓. Memory tip: in MCQs, testing each option in both equations is often fastest.",
 "|If f(x) = 2x² + 3x − 1, find f(−2).|−3|1|−1|3": "f(−2) = 2(−2)^{2} + 3(−2) − 1 = 8 − 6 − 1 = 1: B. Trap: A (−3) comes from (−2)^{2} = −4, a sign mistake. Memory tip: put negative numbers in brackets before squaring.",
 "|A box contains 5 red, 3 blue and 2 green balls. A ball is picked at random. What is the probability of picking a blue or green ball?|1/2|3/10|2/10|5/10": "Blue or green: 3 + 2 = 5 balls out of 10, so P = 5/10 = 1/2: A. Option D (5/10) is the same value, so it is also accepted. Trap: B counts only blue. Memory tip: \"or\" with mutually exclusive outcomes → add the counts.",
 "|What is the locus of a point equidistant from two fixed points A and B?|The perpendicular bisector of AB|The bisector of angle A|A circle through A and B|A line through A": "Every point that is the same distance from A and from B lies on the perpendicular bisector of AB (the line through the midpoint, at right angles to AB): A. Memory tip: equidistant from two points → perpendicular bisector; from two lines → angle bisector.",
 "|Given matrix M = [[2,1],[3,4]], find its determinant.|5|8|11|14": "For the 2 × 2 matrix with rows (2, 1) and (3, 4), the determinant is ad − bc = 2 × 4 − 1 × 3 = 8 − 3 = 5: A. Trap: C (11) adds instead of subtracting. Memory tip: determinant = main diagonal product minus the other diagonal product.",
 "|Simplify: (x² − 4)/(x − 2)|x + 2|x − 2|x² + 2x|x − 4": "Factorise the top as a difference of two squares: x^{2} − 4 = (x + 2)(x − 2). Cancel (x − 2): x + 2: A. Trap: you can't cancel terms like x^{2}/x; factorise first. Memory tip: a^{2} − b^{2} = (a + b)(a − b).",
 "|Find the value of angle x in a triangle where the exterior angle is 110° and one non-adjacent interior angle is 65°.|45°|55°|70°|75°": "An exterior angle equals the sum of the two interior opposite angles: 110° = 65° + x → x = 45°: A. Trap: C (70°) is the adjacent interior angle, 180° − 110°. Memory tip: exterior angle = sum of the two \"far\" interior angles.",
 "|The histogram shows frequency distribution. If the bars represent frequencies 4, 7, 10, 6, 3 for intervals 1-5, 6-10, 11-15, 16-20, 21-25 respectively, what is the modal class?|6-10|11-15|1-5|16-20": "The modal class is the class with the highest frequency: 10, for 11–15: B. Memory tip: mode = most frequent; in a histogram, the tallest bar.",
 "|Express 0.000372 in standard form.|3.72 × 10⁻⁴|3.72 × 10⁻³|37.2 × 10⁻⁵|0.372 × 10⁻³": "Standard form is A × 10^{n} with 1 ≤ A < 10. Move the point 4 places right: 0.000372 = 3.72 × 10^{−4}: A. C and D are equal in value but not in standard form, because A is not between 1 and 10. Memory tip: count how many places the point moves; moving right gives a negative power.",
 "|Evaluate: 2⁵ × 4⁻² ÷ 8¹|1/4|1/2|1|2": "Write everything as powers of 2: 2^{5} × (2^{2})^{−2} ÷ 2^{3} = 2^{5} × 2^{−4} ÷ 2^{3} = 2^{5 − 4 − 3} = 2^{−2} = 1/4: A. Memory tip: change every base to the same prime, then add and subtract the powers.",
 "|The nth term of a sequence is given by Tₙ = 3n − 1. Find the sum of the first 10 terms.|155|175|145|165": "T_{n} = 3n − 1 is an AP with first term 2 and last term (n = 10) 29. Sum = n/2 (first + last) = 10/2 × (2 + 29) = 5 × 31 = 155: A. Trap: D (165) forgets the −1 in each term. Memory tip: for an AP, S_{n} = n/2 × (first term + last term).",
 "|If x varies directly as y² and x = 12 when y = 2, find x when y = 5.|60|75|90|100": "x varies as y^{2}, so x = ky^{2}. From 12 = k × 4, k = 3. When y = 5, x = 3 × 25 = 75: B. Trap: A (60) treats it as direct variation with y, not y^{2}. Memory tip: find k first.",
 "|Expand (2a − b)⁴ and find the coefficient of a²b².|12|24|16|48": "In (2a − b)^{4}, the a^{2}b^{2} term is ⁴C_{2}(2a)^{2}(−b)^{2} = 6 × 4a^{2} × b^{2} = 24a^{2}b^{2}. Coefficient 24: B. Trap: A (12) forgets to square the 2 in 2a. Memory tip: in the binomial expansion, raise the whole term (2a, not just a) to its power.",
 "|A straight line passes through (0, 3) and has gradient −2. Find the x-intercept.|3/2|2/3|−3/2|6": "The line is y = −2x + 3. At the x-intercept, y = 0: 0 = −2x + 3 → x = 3/2: A. Trap: C (−3/2) is a sign slip. Memory tip: x-intercept → put y = 0; y-intercept → put x = 0.",
 "|Convert 11011₂ to base 10.|25|27|29|31": "11011_{2} = 1×16 + 1×8 + 0×4 + 1×2 + 1×1 = 27: B. Memory tip: place values in base 2 double from the right: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16.",
 "|The ages of students in a class have a standard deviation of 0. This means:|All students have different ages|All students have the same age|The ages are normally distributed|Half are above and half below the mean": "Standard deviation measures spread from the mean. If it is 0, no value differs from the mean, so every student has the same age: B. Memory tip: zero spread = all values identical.",
 "|A cone has base radius 6 cm and height 8 cm. Find its volume. (π = 22/7)|301.7 cm³|302.3 cm³|150.86 cm³|96π cm³": "V = ⅓πr^{2}h = ⅓ × π × 36 × 8 = 96π cm³. With π = 22/7, 96 × 22/7 ≈ 301.7 cm³: A. D (96π cm³) is the same volume in exact form, so it is also accepted. Trap: C halves the volume. Memory tip: a cone is one-third of the cylinder with the same base and height.",
 "|Which of the following sets is a subset of {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}?|{1, 6}|{2, 4, 6}|{3, 5}|{0, 2, 4}": "A subset may contain only elements of the parent set. {3, 5} uses only elements of {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}: C. A and B contain 6, and D contains 0, which are not in the set. Memory tip: one \"outside\" element is enough to stop a set being a subset.",
 "|The interior angle of a regular polygon is 150°. How many sides does it have?|10|12|15|8": "Each exterior angle = 180° − 150° = 30°. Exterior angles add to 360°, so n = 360/30 = 12: B. Memory tip: for regular polygons, work with the exterior angle (360 ÷ n); it is quicker.",
 "|Solve: log₂(x + 3) + log₂(x − 1) = 5|x = 5|x = 6|x = 3|x = 4": "Combine: log_{2}[(x + 3)(x − 1)] = 5, so (x + 3)(x − 1) = 2^{5} = 32. Then x^{2} + 2x − 35 = 0 → (x + 7)(x − 5) = 0. x = −7 makes the logs undefined, so x = 5: A. Memory tip: always check log solutions; the inside of every log must be positive.",
 "|If P and Q are two events and P(P) = 0.4, P(Q) = 0.3, P(P∩Q) = 0.1, find P(P∪Q).|0.6|0.7|0.5|0.8": "P(P ∪ Q) = P(P) + P(Q) − P(P ∩ Q) = 0.4 + 0.3 − 0.1 = 0.6: A. Trap: B (0.7) forgets to subtract the overlap. Memory tip: \"or\" = add, then subtract the \"and\" so it isn't counted twice.",
 "|Without using tables, evaluate (343)^{1/3} × (0.14)^{-1} × (25)^{-1/2}.|10|12|8|7": "Step 1: (343)^{1/3} is the cube root of 343, which is 7.\nStep 2: (0.14)^{-1} = 1/0.14 = 100/14 = 50/7.\nStep 3: (25)^{-1/2} = 1/√25 = 1/5.\nStep 4: Multiply: 7 × 50/7 × 1/5 = 50/5 = 10: A.\nTrap: 7 (D) is just the first factor; reading (25)^{-1/2} as 5 instead of 1/5 gives 250, so watch every negative power.\nMemory tip: a^{-n} = 1/a^{n} and a^{1/n} is the nth root; do the root first, then the power.",
 "|In a school, 220 students offer Biology or Mathematics or both, 125 offer Biology and 110 offer Mathematics. How many offer Biology but not Mathematics?|95|80|125|110": "Step 1: Let both = k. Then n(B ∪ M) = n(B) + n(M) − k, so 220 = 125 + 110 − k, giving k = 15.\nStep 2: Biology but not Mathematics = 125 − 15 = 110: D.\nShortcut: everyone in the union who does not offer Mathematics offers Biology only, so 220 − 110 = 110.\nTrap: 125 (C) is all Biology students, including the 15 who also offer Mathematics; 95 (A) comes from 110 − 15, which is Mathematics only.\nMemory tip: \"A but not B\" = n(A ∪ B) − n(B) = n(A) − n(A ∩ B).",
 "|Simplify 52.4 − 5.7 − 3.45 − 1.75.|41.4|41.5|42.1|42.2": "Step 1: Add everything being subtracted: 5.7 + 3.45 + 1.75 = 10.9.\nStep 2: 52.4 − 10.9 = 41.5: B.\nTrap: subtracting one at a time invites slips with the decimal places (for example 46.7 − 3.45 written as 43.35 instead of 43.25). Write each number to two decimal places (52.40, 5.70, 3.45, 1.75) before working.\nMemory tip: when several numbers are subtracted from one number, add the small ones first and subtract once; fewer steps means fewer slips.",
 "|Simplify (√0.7 + √70)^{2}.|84.7|70.7|217.7|168.7": "Step 1: Expand (a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b² with a = √0.7, b = √70.\nStep 2: a² = 0.7 and b² = 70.\nStep 3: 2ab = 2√(0.7 × 70) = 2√49 = 2 × 7 = 14.\nStep 4: Total = 0.7 + 14 + 70 = 84.7: A.\nTrap: 70.7 (B) comes from writing (a + b)² = a² + b² and forgetting the middle term 2ab.\nMemory tip: √p × √q = √(pq); look for a product under the root that is a perfect square.",
 "|Evaluate (0.21 × 0.072 × 0.00054)/(0.006 × 1.68 × 0.063), correct to four significant figures.|0.01286|0.01285|0.1286|0.1285": "Step 1: Pair numbers that divide neatly: 0.21/0.063 = 10/3 and 0.072/0.006 = 12.\nStep 2: That leaves 0.00054/1.68 = 0.000321428…\nStep 3: 10/3 × 12 = 40, and 40 × 0.000321428… = 0.0128571… (exactly 9/700).\nStep 4: To four significant figures: 0.01286 (the next digit, 7, rounds the 5 up): A.\nTrap: 0.01285 (B) truncates instead of rounding; 0.1286 (C) slips the decimal point by one place.\nMemory tip: significant figures start at the first non-zero digit; leading zeros never count.",
 "|A trader bought goats for ₦4,000 each. He sold them for ₦180,000 at a loss of 25%. How many goats did he buy?|60|50|45|36": "Step 1: A 25% loss means he sold at 75% of the cost price.\nStep 2: Cost price = 180,000 ÷ 0.75 = ₦240,000.\nStep 3: Number of goats = 240,000 ÷ 4,000 = 60: A.\nTrap: 45 (C) comes from dividing the selling price by the unit cost (180,000 ÷ 4,000); the ₦4,000 is a buying price, so it must divide the total cost, not the takings.\nMemory tip: after a loss of r%, SP = (100 − r)% of CP, so CP = SP × 100/(100 − r).",
 "|If dy/dx = 2x − 3 and y = 3 when x = 0, find y in terms of x.|2x^{2} − 3x|x^{2} − 3x|x^{2} − 3x − 3|x^{2} − 3x + 3": "Step 1: Integrate: y = ∫(2x − 3) dx = x² − 3x + c.\nStep 2: Use y = 3 when x = 0: 3 = 0 − 0 + c, so c = 3.\nStep 3: y = x² − 3x + 3: D.\nTrap: B (x² − 3x) forgets the constant; A (2x² − 3x) forgets to divide by the new power when integrating 2x.\nMemory tip: every indefinite integral has a \"+ c\"; a given point (x, y) is there to find it.",
 "|Find the derivative of y = sin^{2}(5x) with respect to x.|10 sin 5x cos 5x|5 sin 5x cos 5x|2 sin 5x cos 5x|15 sin 5x cos 5x": "Step 1: Write y = (sin 5x)², an outer square of an inner sin 5x.\nStep 2: Outer derivative: 2 sin 5x.\nStep 3: Inner derivative: d/dx(sin 5x) = 5 cos 5x.\nStep 4: Multiply: 2 sin 5x × 5 cos 5x = 10 sin 5x cos 5x: A.\nTrap: C (2 sin 5x cos 5x) misses the 5 from inside the sine; B uses the 5 but forgets the 2 from the square.\nMemory tip: chain rule = derivative of the outside × derivative of the inside, for every layer. (10 sin 5x cos 5x also equals 5 sin 10x.)",
 "|The slope of the tangent to the curve y = 3x^{2} − 2x + 5 at the point (1, 6) is|4|1|6|5": "Step 1: The slope of the tangent is dy/dx.\nStep 2: dy/dx = 6x − 2.\nStep 3: At (1, 6), x = 1: slope = 6 − 2 = 4: A.\nTrap: 6 (C) is the y-coordinate of the point, not the slope; substituting y = 6 or x into the curve itself does not give a gradient.\nMemory tip: gradient of a curve at a point = dy/dx evaluated at that point's x-value.",
 "|Evaluate ∫ sin 3x dx.|(2/3) cos 3x + C|(1/2) cos 3x + C|−(1/3) cos 3x + C|−(2/3) cos 3x + C": "Step 1: The derivative of cos 3x is −3 sin 3x.\nStep 2: So ∫ sin 3x dx must be −(1/3) cos 3x + C, because differentiating it gives −(1/3)(−3 sin 3x) = sin 3x.\nAnswer: C.\nTrap: A and D keep the wrong sign or the wrong fraction; B ignores the 3 entirely. Always check your answer by differentiating it back.\nMemory tip: ∫ sin ax dx = −(1/a) cos ax + C and ∫ cos ax dx = (1/a) sin ax + C.",
 "|A circle with a radius 5 cm has its radius increasing at the rate of 0.2 cm s^{-1}. What will be the corresponding increase in the area?|2π|5π|π|4π": "Step 1: Area A = πr².\nStep 2: Differentiate with respect to time: dA/dt = 2πr × dr/dt.\nStep 3: Substitute r = 5 and dr/dt = 0.2: dA/dt = 2π × 5 × 0.2 = 2π cm² per second: A.\nTrap: forgetting the 2 in 2πr gives π (C); leaving out dr/dt gives 10π. Always multiply by dr/dt.\nMemory tip: connected rates: dA/dt = (dA/dr) × (dr/dt).",
 "|If y = x^{2} − 1/x, find dy/dx.|2x − 1/x^{2}|2x + x^{2}|2x − x^{2}|2x + 1/x^{2}": "Step 1: Rewrite with a power: y = x² − x^{-1}.\nStep 2: d/dx(x²) = 2x.\nStep 3: d/dx(−x^{-1}) = −(−1)x^{-2} = +x^{-2} = 1/x².\nStep 4: dy/dx = 2x + 1/x²: D.\nTrap: A (2x − 1/x²) forgets that the two minus signs multiply to plus.\nMemory tip: change 1/x^{n} to x^{-n} before differentiating, then the power rule handles the sign for you.",
 "|Use the graph above (the boundary line passes through (−4, 0) and (0, 2), and the shaded region contains the origin) to find the value of p and q if px + qy ≤ 4.|p = 2, q = 1|p = 2, q = −1|p = 1, q = 2|p = −1, q = 2": "Step 1: The boundary line passes through (−4, 0) and (0, 2): gradient 2/4 = 1/2, intercept 2, so y = x/2 + 2.\nStep 2: Multiply by 2: 2y = x + 4, i.e. −x + 2y = 4.\nStep 3: The shaded side contains the origin, and 0 ≤ 4 is true, so the region is −x + 2y ≤ 4.\nStep 4: Compare with px + qy ≤ 4: p = −1, q = 2: D.\nTrap: C (p = 1, q = 2) loses the minus sign; test it at (−4, 0): −4 ≠ 4.\nMemory tip: check a boundary line by substituting both marked points.",
 "|Solve for x in the equation x^{3} − 5x^{2} − x + 5 = 0.|1, −1 or 5|1, 1 or −5|−1, 1 or −5|1, 1 or 5": "Step 1: Group in pairs: x²(x − 5) − 1(x − 5) = 0.\nStep 2: Factor: (x − 5)(x² − 1) = (x − 5)(x − 1)(x + 1) = 0.\nStep 3: x = 5, 1 or −1: A.\nCheck: f(1) = 1 − 5 − 1 + 5 = 0 and f(−1) = −1 − 5 + 1 + 5 = 0.\nTrap: C has −5 instead of 5; the sign of the root is opposite to the sign in the factor (x − 5).\nMemory tip: for a cubic with four terms, try grouping first, or test x = ±1 as factors.",
 "|The time taken to do a piece of work is inversely proportional to the number of men employed. If it takes 45 men to do a piece of work in 5 days, how long will it take 25 men?|15 days|12 days|5 days|9 days": "Step 1: Inverse proportion: t = k/m, so t × m stays constant.\nStep 2: k = 45 × 5 = 225 man-days.\nStep 3: With 25 men: t = 225 ÷ 25 = 9 days: D.\nTrap: with fewer men the job must take LONGER than 5 days, so C (5 days) is impossible; 15 days (A) is a guess that does not satisfy t × m = 225.\nMemory tip: inverse variation means the product is constant; direct variation means the ratio is constant.",
 "|If P = [[2, 1], [−3, 0]] and I is a 2 × 2 unit matrix, evaluate P^{2} − 2P + 4I.|[[9, 4], [−12, 1]]|[[−3, 0], [0, −4]]|[[1, 0], [0, 1]]|[[1, 4], [4, 1]]": "Step 1: P² = [[2, 1], [−3, 0]] × [[2, 1], [−3, 0]] = [[4 − 3, 2 + 0], [−6 + 0, −3 + 0]] = [[1, 2], [−6, −3]].\nStep 2: 2P = [[4, 2], [−6, 0]], so P² − 2P = [[−3, 0], [0, −3]].\nStep 3: Add 4I = [[4, 0], [0, 4]]: result [[1, 0], [0, 1]]: C.\nTrap: squaring each entry (giving [[4, 1], [9, 0]]) is not matrix multiplication; multiply row by column.\nMemory tip: (AB)_{ij} = row i of A times column j of B, entry by entry, then add.",
 "|Find the range of values of x for which (x + 2)/4 − (2x − 3)/3 < 4.|x > −6|x > −3|x < 8|x < 4": "Step 1: Multiply every term by 12 (the LCM of 4 and 3): 3(x + 2) − 4(2x − 3) < 48.\nStep 2: Expand: 3x + 6 − 8x + 12 < 48, so −5x + 18 < 48.\nStep 3: −5x < 30.\nStep 4: Divide by −5 and reverse the sign: x > −6: A.\nTrap: forgetting to reverse the inequality gives x < −6; writing −4(2x − 3) as −8x − 12 gives a different wrong bound.\nMemory tip: multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number flips the sign.",
 "|Find the maximum value of y in the equation y = 1 − 2x − 3x^{2}.|5/4|5/3|3/4|4/3": "Step 1: dy/dx = −2 − 6x. Set it to zero: x = −1/3.\nStep 2: The coefficient of x² is negative, so this point is a maximum.\nStep 3: y = 1 − 2(−1/3) − 3(1/9) = 1 + 2/3 − 1/3 = 4/3: D.\nTrap: 5/3 (B) is 1 + 2/3, forgetting the −3x² term; remember x² = 1/9, so −3x² = −1/3.\nMemory tip: for y = ax² + bx + c, the turning point is at x = −b/(2a); it is a maximum when a < 0.",
 "|If the 9th term of an A.P. is five times the 5th term, find the relationship between a and d.|2a + d = 0|3a + 5d = 0|a + 3d = 0|a + 2d = 0": "Step 1: T_{n} = a + (n − 1)d, so T_{9} = a + 8d and T_{5} = a + 4d.\nStep 2: a + 8d = 5(a + 4d) = 5a + 20d.\nStep 3: 0 = 4a + 12d, divide by 4: a + 3d = 0: C.\nTrap: expanding 5(a + 4d) as 5a + 4d loses the relationship; multiply every term in the bracket by 5.\nMemory tip: the nth term of an AP is a + (n − 1)d; the first term already counts as one.",
 "|Make r the subject of the formula x/(r + a) = a/r.|a^{2}/(x − a)|a^{2}/(x + a)|a/(x − a)|a/(x + a)": "Step 1: Cross-multiply: xr = a(r + a) = ar + a².\nStep 2: Collect r-terms: xr − ar = a².\nStep 3: Factor r: r(x − a) = a².\nStep 4: r = a²/(x − a): A.\nTrap: B has x + a, a sign slip when moving ar to the other side; C and D lose the square on a.\nMemory tip: to make a letter the subject when it appears twice, gather all its terms on one side and factor it out.",
 "|The inverse of the function f(x) = 3x + 4 is|(1/3)(x − 4)|(1/5)(x − 5)|(1/4)(x + 3)|(1/3)(x + 4)": "Step 1: Let y = 3x + 4.\nStep 2: Solve for x: 3x = y − 4, so x = (y − 4)/3.\nStep 3: Swap letters: f^{-1}(x) = (1/3)(x − 4): A.\nCheck: f(f^{-1}(x)) = 3 × (x − 4)/3 + 4 = x.\nTrap: D (1/3)(x + 4) undoes the multiplication but not the addition correctly; inverse operations are done in reverse order with opposite signs.\nMemory tip: inverse = undo the last step first (\"subtract 4, then divide by 3\").",
 "|If −2 is the solution of the equation 2x + 1 − 3c = 2c + 3x − 7, find the value of c.|4|3|2|1": "Step 1: Substitute x = −2: 2(−2) + 1 − 3c = 2c + 3(−2) − 7.\nStep 2: Left: −3 − 3c. Right: 2c − 13.\nStep 3: −3 + 13 = 2c + 3c, so 10 = 5c.\nStep 4: c = 2: C.\nTrap: putting x = +2 instead of −2 gives a different value; keep the negative sign inside brackets when substituting.\nMemory tip: \"x = −2 is a solution\" means the equation is true when x = −2, so substitute and solve for the other letter.",
 "|The binary operation * is defined on the set of integers p and q by p * q = pq + p + q. Find 2 * (3 * 4).|59|19|67|38": "Step 1: Work the bracket first: 3 * 4 = 3 × 4 + 3 + 4 = 19.\nStep 2: Now 2 * 19 = 2 × 19 + 2 + 19 = 38 + 21 = 59: A.\nTrap: 19 (B) stops after the bracket; 38 (D) computes only the product 2 × 19.\nMemory tip: with a defined operation, replace p and q in the rule exactly, and evaluate the innermost bracket first.",
 "|If N = [[3, 5, −4], [6, −3, −5], [−2, 2, 1]], find |N|.|17|23|65|91": "Step 1: Expand along the first row: |N| = 3|−3 −5; 2 1| − 5|6 −5; −2 1| + (−4)|6 −3; −2 2|.\nStep 2: Minors: (−3)(1) − (−5)(2) = 7; (6)(1) − (−5)(−2) = −4; (6)(2) − (−3)(−2) = 6.\nStep 3: |N| = 3(7) − 5(−4) − 4(6) = 21 + 20 − 24 = 17: A.\nTrap: forgetting the alternating signs + − + gives 21 − 20 − 24 = −23, which tempts you towards 23 (B).\nMemory tip: 3 × 3 determinant: a(ei − fh) − b(di − fg) + c(dh − eg).",
 "|The sum to infinity of the series 1 + 1/3 + 1/9 + 1/27 + … is|11/3|10/3|5/2|3/2": "Step 1: Each term is 1/3 of the previous one, so it is a GP with a = 1 and r = 1/3.\nStep 2: |r| < 1, so the sum to infinity exists: S∞ = a/(1 − r) = 1/(2/3) = 3/2: D.\nTrap: 5/2 (C) and 10/3 (B) are too big: the partial sums 1, 4/3, 13/9, 40/27 creep towards 3/2 but never pass it.\nMemory tip: S∞ = a/(1 − r), valid only when −1 < r < 1.",
 "|If x varies directly as √n and x = 9 when n = 9, find x when n = 17/9.|4|27|√3|√17": "Step 1: x = k√n.\nStep 2: 9 = k√9 = 3k, so k = 3.\nStep 3: n = 17/9: √n = √17/3, so x = 3 × √17/3 = √17: D.\nTrap: writing √(17/9) as √17/9 forgets to root the 9; the denominator becomes 3, which cancels with k = 3.\nMemory tip: write the variation equation first (x = k√n), find k, then substitute; √(a/b) = √a/√b.",
 "|In the diagram above, PST is a straight line, PQ = QS = RS. If ∠RST = 72°, find x.|36°|18°|72°|24°": "Step 1: PQ = QS, so △PQS is isosceles: ∠QSP = ∠QPS = x.\nStep 2: The exterior angle of △PQS at Q is ∠SQR = x + x = 2x.\nStep 3: QS = RS, so △QSR is isosceles: ∠SRQ = ∠SQR = 2x.\nStep 4: In △PRS, the exterior angle at S is ∠RST = ∠SPR + ∠SRP = x + 2x = 3x.\nStep 5: 3x = 72°, so x = 24°: D.\nTrap: 36° (A) comes from 72 ÷ 2, as if only one isosceles triangle mattered.\nMemory tip: exterior angle of a triangle = sum of the two interior opposite angles.",
 "|A chord of a circle subtends an angle of 120° at the centre of a circle of diameter 4√3 cm. Calculate the area of the major sector.|4π cm²|32π cm²|16π cm²|8π cm²": "Step 1: Diameter 4√3, so r = 2√3 and r² = 4 × 3 = 12.\nStep 2: The minor sector has angle 120°, so the MAJOR sector has 360° − 120° = 240°.\nStep 3: Area = (240/360) × π × 12 = (2/3) × 12π = 8π cm²: D.\nTrap: 4π (A) is the minor sector; 16π (C) comes from squaring 2√3 as 24 instead of 4 × 3 = 12.\nMemory tip: sector area = (θ/360) × πr²; major angle = 360° − minor angle.",
 "|Find the equation of the set of points which are equidistant from the parallel lines x = 1 and x = 7.|y = 3|x = 3|x = 4|y = 4": "Step 1: The lines x = 1 and x = 7 are vertical and parallel.\nStep 2: Points equidistant from both lie on the vertical line halfway between them.\nStep 3: x = (1 + 7)/2 = 4: C.\nTrap: \"y = ...\" options (A, D) are horizontal lines, which cannot run parallel between two vertical lines; x = 3 (B) is half the gap (6 ÷ 2), not the midpoint.\nMemory tip: the locus equidistant from two parallel lines is the parallel line midway between them.",
 "|In the diagram above, XZ is the diameter of the circle XYZW, with centre O and radius 15/2 cm. If XY = 12 cm, find the area of the triangle XYZ.|54 cm²|45 cm²|27 cm²|75 cm²": "Step 1: XZ is a diameter: XZ = 2 × 15/2 = 15 cm.\nStep 2: The angle in a semicircle is 90°, so ∠XYZ = 90°.\nStep 3: Pythagoras: YZ = √(15² − 12²) = √81 = 9 cm.\nStep 4: Area = ½ × XY × YZ = ½ × 12 × 9 = 54 cm²: A.\nTrap: 27 (C) halves twice; 45 (B) uses the radius 7.5 as a side. The right angle is at Y, so XY and YZ are the base and height.\nMemory tip: a triangle drawn on a diameter is always right-angled at the third point.",
 "|If tan θ = 4/3, calculate sin^{2} θ − cos^{2} θ.|16/25|24/25|7/25|9/25": "Step 1: tan θ = 4/3 = opposite/adjacent; the hypotenuse is √(4² + 3²) = 5.\nStep 2: sin θ = 4/5, cos θ = 3/5.\nStep 3: sin² θ − cos² θ = 16/25 − 9/25 = 7/25: C.\nTrap: 16/25 (A) is sin² θ alone; 24/25 (B) is 2 sin θ cos θ (that is sin 2θ, not what is asked).\nMemory tip: from any tan ratio, draw the right triangle and use Pythagoras to get sin and cos.",
 "|In the diagram above are two concentric circles of radii r and R respectively with centre O. If r = (2/5)R, express the area of the shaded portion in terms of π and R.|(21/25)πR^{2}|(9/25)πR^{2}|(21/23)πR^{2}|(5/9)πR^{2}": "Step 1: The shaded ring = big circle − small circle = πR² − πr².\nStep 2: r = (2/5)R, so r² = (4/25)R².\nStep 3: πR² − (4/25)πR² = (21/25)πR²: A.\nTrap: 9/25 (B) uses (1 − 2/5)² instead of 1 − (2/5)²; remember to square r before subtracting.\nMemory tip: area of a ring (annulus) = π(R² − r²) = π(R − r)(R + r).",
 "|A bucket is 12 cm in diameter at the top, 8 cm in diameter at the bottom and 4 cm deep. Calculate its volume.|(304/3)π cm³|144π cm³|(128/3)π cm³|72π cm³": "Step 1: A bucket is a frustum: V = (πh/3)(R² + Rr + r²).\nStep 2: R = 6 cm (top), r = 4 cm (bottom), h = 4 cm.\nStep 3: V = (4π/3)(36 + 24 + 16) = (4π/3)(76) = (304/3)π cm³: A.\nTrap: using diameters instead of radii, or (π/3)h(R² + r²) without the Rr term, gives the other options. The paper prints the units as cm²; volume is in cm³, which we have corrected.\nMemory tip: frustum volume = (πh/3)(R² + Rr + r²); always halve diameters first.",
 "|In the diagram above, a cylinder is surmounted by a hemispherical bowl. Calculate the volume of the solid.|180π cm³|162π cm³|216π cm³|198π cm³": "Step 1: From the diagram, the hemisphere radius (and cylinder radius) is 3 cm and the cylinder height is 20 cm.\nStep 2: Cylinder: πr²h = π × 9 × 20 = 180π.\nStep 3: Hemisphere: (2/3)πr³ = (2/3)π × 27 = 18π.\nStep 4: Total = 198π cm³: D.\nTrap: 180π (A) leaves out the hemisphere; 216π (C) uses a full sphere (36π) instead of half; 162π (B) subtracts the hemisphere.\nMemory tip: hemisphere volume = (2/3)πr³, half of the sphere's (4/3)πr³.",
 "|The sum of the interior angles of a polygon is 20 right angles. How many sides does the polygon have?|12|20|40|10": "Step 1: Sum of interior angles = (2n − 4) right angles.\nStep 2: 2n − 4 = 20, so 2n = 24 and n = 12: A.\n(Same as (n − 2) × 180° = 1800°, giving n − 2 = 10.)\nTrap: 10 (D) is n − 2, the number of triangles, not the number of sides; 20 (B) just copies the number of right angles.\nMemory tip: interior angle sum = (n − 2) × 180°; exterior angles always total 360°.",
 "|Find the coordinate of the midpoint of the x and y intercepts of the line 2y = 4x − 8.|(2, 0)|(1, −2)|(−1, −2)|(1, 2)": "Step 1: Write the line as y = 2x − 4.\nStep 2: x-intercept: y = 0 gives x = 2, point (2, 0).\nStep 3: y-intercept: x = 0 gives y = −4, point (0, −4).\nStep 4: Midpoint = ((2 + 0)/2, (0 − 4)/2) = (1, −2): B.\nTrap: (2, 0) (A) is just the x-intercept; (1, 2) (D) loses the minus sign on the y-intercept.\nMemory tip: intercepts: set the other variable to zero; midpoint = average of the coordinates.",
 "|A hunter 1.6 m tall views a bird on top of a tree at an angle of 45°. If the distance between the hunter and the tree is 10.4 m, find the height of the tree.|9.0 m|12.0 m|8.8 m|10.4 m": "Step 1: From the hunter's eye, the rise to the bird = 10.4 × tan 45° = 10.4 × 1 = 10.4 m.\nStep 2: The eye is 1.6 m above the ground, so the tree is 10.4 + 1.6 = 12.0 m tall: B.\nTrap: 10.4 m (D) forgets the hunter's height; 8.8 m (C) subtracts it instead of adding.\nMemory tip: in elevation problems, the angle is measured from eye level, so add the observer's height at the end.",
 "|A solid hemisphere has radius 7 cm. Find the total surface area. [π = 22/7]|400 m²|462 cm²|66 cm²|308 cm²": "Step 1: A SOLID hemisphere has a curved surface and a flat circular base.\nStep 2: Curved surface = 2πr²; base = πr²; total = 3πr².\nStep 3: 3 × 22/7 × 49 = 3 × 154 = 462 cm²: B.\nTrap: 308 (D) is the curved surface only (2πr²); 66 (C) is 3πr, forgetting to square r.\nMemory tip: hemisphere: curved 2πr², solid total 3πr²; sphere 4πr².",
 "|Find the value of α if the line 2y − αx + 4 = 0 is perpendicular to the line y + (1/4)x − 7 = 0.|−4|4|8|−8": "Step 1: 2y − αx + 4 = 0 gives y = (α/2)x − 2, gradient α/2.\nStep 2: y + (1/4)x − 7 = 0 gives y = −(1/4)x + 7, gradient −1/4.\nStep 3: Perpendicular: (α/2) × (−1/4) = −1, so −α/8 = −1 and α = 8: C.\nTrap: −8 (D) uses product = +1; 4 or −4 forgets the 2 in front of y.\nMemory tip: perpendicular gradients multiply to −1 (m₂ = −1/m₁); always make y the subject first.",
 "|The triangle PQR above is|an obtuse-angled triangle|a scalene triangle|an isosceles triangle|an equilateral triangle": "Step 1: The 128° is an exterior angle at R (between RP and QR produced), so ∠PRQ = 180° − 128° = 52°.\nStep 2: ∠P = 180° − 76° − 52° = 52°.\nStep 3: ∠P = ∠R, so the triangle has two equal angles (and QP = QR): isosceles: C.\nTrap: \"obtuse-angled\" (A) mistakes the 128° for an interior angle; all three interior angles here are acute.\nMemory tip: angles on a straight line add to 180°; two equal angles mean two equal sides.",
 "|The locus of a point P which is equidistant from two given points S and T is|the perpendicular bisector of ST|the angle bisector of PS and ST|a perpendicular to ST|a line parallel to ST": "Step 1: Any point equally far from S and T lies on the line that cuts ST in half at right angles.\nStep 2: So the locus is the perpendicular bisector of ST: A.\nTrap: \"a perpendicular to ST\" (C) is not enough; it must pass through the midpoint of ST. The angle bisector (B) is the locus equidistant from two LINES, not two points.\nMemory tip: equidistant from two points = perpendicular bisector; equidistant from two lines = angle bisector.",
 "|Find the mean of the data 7, −3, 4, −2, 5, −9, 4, 8, −6, 12.|3|4|1|2": "Step 1: Add the positives: 7 + 4 + 5 + 4 + 8 + 12 = 40.\nStep 2: Add the negatives: 3 + 2 + 9 + 6 = 20, so the total is 40 − 20 = 20.\nStep 3: There are 10 values: mean = 20 ÷ 10 = 2: D.\nTrap: 4 (B) or 3 (A) come from dropping a minus sign or miscounting the values; count them before dividing.\nMemory tip: with signed data, sum positives and negatives separately, then combine.",
 "|The Venn diagram above shows the number of students offering Music and History in a class of 80 students. If a student is picked at random from the class, what is the probability that he offers Music only?|0.38|0.13|0.50|0.25": "Step 1: All regions add to 80: (30 − x) + x + (40 − x) + 20 = 80.\nStep 2: 90 − x = 80, so x = 10.\nStep 3: Music only = 30 − x = 20.\nStep 4: P(Music only) = 20/80 = 0.25: D.\nTrap: 0.38 (A) uses all Music students (30/80); \"only\" excludes the 10 in the overlap.\nMemory tip: in a Venn diagram, first find the unknown by making all regions total the universal set.",
 "|The range of the data k + 2, k − 3, k + 4, k − 2, k − 5, k + 3, k − 1 and k + 6 is|10|11|6|8": "Step 1: Range = largest − smallest.\nStep 2: Largest value is k + 6; smallest is k − 5.\nStep 3: (k + 6) − (k − 5) = 11; the k cancels: B.\nTrap: 8 (D) is the number of values, not the range; and the first and last listed values are not the largest and smallest, so sort mentally first.\nMemory tip: adding the same k to every value shifts the data but never changes the range.",
 "|The probability of a student passing any examination is 2/3. If the student takes three examinations, what is the probability that he will not pass any of them?|2/3|4/9|8/27|1/27": "Step 1: P(not passing one exam) = 1 − 2/3 = 1/3.\nStep 2: The exams are independent, so P(fails all three) = (1/3)³ = 1/27: D.\nTrap: 8/27 (C) is the probability of passing all three, (2/3)³; read \"not pass any\" carefully.\nMemory tip: \"none\" = (probability of failure)^{n}; \"at least one\" = 1 − P(none).",
 "|The acres for rice, pineapple, cassava, cocoa and palm oil in a certain district are given respectively as 2, 5, 3, 11 and 9. What is the angle of the sector for cassava in a pie chart?|100°|180°|36°|60°": "Step 1: Total acres = 2 + 5 + 3 + 11 + 9 = 30.\nStep 2: Cassava sector = (3/30) × 360° = 36°: C.\nTrap: 60° (D) uses pineapple (5/30 × 360°); match the data in the order given (\"respectively\").\nMemory tip: sector angle = (item/total) × 360°; all sectors must add up to 360°.",
 "|How many three-digit numbers can be formed from 32564 without any digit being repeated?|120|10|20|60": "Step 1: The digits 3, 2, 5, 6, 4 are five different digits.\nStep 2: Three-digit numbers with no repetition: 5 choices for the first place, 4 for the second, 3 for the third.\nStep 3: 5 × 4 × 3 = 60 = ⁵P₃: D.\nTrap: 10 (B) is ⁵C₃, which ignores order, but 325 and 523 are different numbers; 120 (A) is 5!.\nMemory tip: when order matters (numbers, arrangements), use permutations ⁿP_{r}; when it does not (committees), use combinations.",
 "|The mean of a set of six numbers is 60. If the mean of the first five is 50, find the sixth number in the set.|105|100|95|110": "Step 1: Total of six numbers = 6 × 60 = 360.\nStep 2: Total of first five = 5 × 50 = 250.\nStep 3: Sixth number = 360 − 250 = 110: D.\nTrap: averaging the means or guessing near 60 misleads; the sixth number must make up 6 × 60 − 5 × 50 = 110, so work with totals, not means.\nMemory tip: total = mean × number of items; adding or removing items is easiest with totals.",
 "|Calculate the mean deviation of the set of numbers 7, 3, 14, 9, 7 and 8.|21/6|2½|11/6|2⅓": "Step 1: Mean = (7 + 3 + 14 + 9 + 7 + 8) ÷ 6 = 48 ÷ 6 = 8.\nStep 2: Absolute deviations from 8: 1, 5, 6, 1, 1, 0; total = 14.\nStep 3: Mean deviation = 14 ÷ 6 = 7/3 = 2⅓: D.\nTrap: forgetting to take absolute values makes the deviations sum to 0; divide by the number of values (6), not by 5.\nMemory tip: mean deviation = Σ|x − x̄| ÷ n, always using the absolute value.",
 "|No. of days: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6; No. of students: 20, x, 50, 40, 2x, 60. The distribution above shows the number of days a group of 260 students were absent from school in a particular term. How many students were absent from school for at least four days in the term?|210|40|120|160": "Step 1: All students add to 260: 20 + x + 50 + 40 + 2x + 60 = 260.\nStep 2: 170 + 3x = 260, so x = 30 and 2x = 60.\nStep 3: \"At least four days\" means 4, 5 or 6 days: 40 + 60 + 60 = 160: D.\nTrap: 40 (B) counts only 4 days; 120 (C) counts 5 and 6 days only, leaving out 4.\nMemory tip: \"at least n\" includes n itself; \"more than n\" does not.",
 "|Evaluate log_{√2} 4 + log_{1/2} 16 − log_{4} 32.|−5.5|−2.5|2.5|5.5": "Step 1: log_{√2} 4: (√2)^{4} = 4, so it is 4. Step 2: log_{1/2} 16: (1/2)^{−4} = 2^{4} = 16, so it is −4. Step 3: log_{4} 32: write both as powers of 2, 2^{2k} = 2^{5}, so k = 5/2. Step 4: 4 + (−4) − 2.5 = −2.5: B. The trap: taking log_{1/2} 16 as +4 gives 5.5 (D) or forgetting the minus sign gives 2.5 (C). Memory tip: to find log_{a} b, write a and b as powers of the same base and compare exponents.",
 "|Simplify 213_{4} × 23_{4}.|10311_{4}|10321_{4}|12231_{4}|13211_{4}": "Step 1: Convert to base ten: 213_{4} = 2×16 + 1×4 + 3 = 39 and 23_{4} = 2×4 + 3 = 11. Step 2: 39 × 11 = 429. Step 3: Convert back by dividing by 4 repeatedly: 429 = 107 r 1, 107 = 26 r 3, 26 = 6 r 2, 6 = 1 r 2, 1 = 0 r 1. Reading the remainders upwards gives 12231_{4}: C. The trap: multiplying digit by digit as in base ten, without carrying in fours, gives the other options. Memory tip: in base work, convert to base ten, do the sum, then convert back.",
 "|In a class of 40 students, 32 offer Mathematics, 24 offer Physics and 4 offer neither Mathematics nor Physics. How many offer both Mathematics and Physics?|20|16|8|4": "Step 1: 4 students take neither, so 40 − 4 = 36 take at least one subject. Step 2: n(M ∪ P) = n(M) + n(P) − n(both), so 36 = 32 + 24 − n(both). Step 3: n(both) = 56 − 36 = 20: A. The trap: forgetting to remove the 4 who take neither gives 56 − 40 = 16 (B). Memory tip: total − neither = n(A ∪ B) = n(A) + n(B) − n(A ∩ B).",
 "|Find (1/0.06 ÷ 1/0.042)^{−1}, correct to two decimal places.|1.43|1.53|3.14|4.42": "Step 1: Dividing by a fraction means multiplying by its reciprocal: 1/0.06 ÷ 1/0.042 = 1/0.06 × 0.042 = 0.042/0.06. Step 2: 0.042/0.06 = 42/60 = 0.7. Step 3: The power −1 means the reciprocal: 1/0.7 = 1.428…, which is 1.43 to two decimal places: A. The trap: ignoring the power −1 leaves 0.7, which is not offered, so some students guess. Memory tip: x^{−1} = 1/x, so flip the final answer.",
 "|A woman buys 270 oranges for ₦1800.00 and sells at 5 for ₦40.00. What is her profit?|₦216.00|₦1620.00|₦630.00|₦360.00": "Step 1: Selling price of one orange = ₦40 ÷ 5 = ₦8. Step 2: Total sales = 270 × ₦8 = ₦2160. Step 3: Profit = selling price − cost price = 2160 − 1800 = ₦360: D. The trap: dividing 1800 by 270 and comparing unit prices badly, or forgetting to subtract the cost, leads to the other options. Memory tip: profit = total selling price − total cost price; find both totals first.",
 "|Simplify 1 − (2⅓ × 1¼) + 3/5.|−1 1/15|−1 19/60|−2 7/15|−2 31/60": "Step 1: Change mixed numbers to improper fractions: 2⅓ = 7/3 and 1¼ = 5/4. Step 2: Bracket first: 7/3 × 5/4 = 35/12. Step 3: 1 − 35/12 + 3/5 with LCM 60: (60 − 175 + 36)/60 = −79/60. Step 4: −79/60 = −1 19/60: B. The trap: working left to right without doing the bracket first, or adding 3/5 to the bracket, gives C or D. Memory tip: BODMAS, and always turn mixed numbers into improper fractions before multiplying.",
 "|Simplify (√98 − √50)/√32.|3|1|½|¼": "Step 1: Simplify each surd using a square factor: √98 = √(49 × 2) = 7√2, √50 = √(25 × 2) = 5√2, √32 = √(16 × 2) = 4√2. Step 2: Numerator: 7√2 − 5√2 = 2√2. Step 3: 2√2/(4√2) = ½: C. The trap: subtracting under the root, √(98 − 50) = √48, gives a messy number that matches nothing. Memory tip: √a − √b ≠ √(a − b); simplify each surd to the same root first.",
 "|A cinema hall contains a certain number of people. If 22½% are children, 47½% are men and 84 are women, find the number of men in the hall.|63|84|113|133": "Step 1: Women's share = 100% − 22½% − 47½% = 30%. Step 2: 30% of the total is 84, so total = 84 × 100/30 = 280. Step 3: Men = 47½% of 280 = 0.475 × 280 = 133: D. The trap: taking 84 as the number of men (B) or using 22½% for the women gives 63 (A). (The paper prints \"cinema half\" for \"cinema hall\".) Memory tip: find the missing percentage first, then use it to get the whole.",
 "|If 9^{2x−1}/27^{x+1} = 1, find the value of x.|8|5|3|2": "Step 1: A fraction equals 1 when top = bottom, so 9^{2x−1} = 27^{x+1}. Step 2: Base 3: (3^{2})^{2x−1} = (3^{3})^{x+1}, so 3^{4x−2} = 3^{3x+3}. Step 3: Equate powers: 4x − 2 = 3x + 3, so x = 5: B. The trap: forgetting to multiply the powers when changing base (writing 3^{2x−1}) gives the wrong equation. Memory tip: (a^{m})^{n} = a^{mn}; get one common base, then equate the indices.",
 "|The sum of four numbers is 1214_{5}. What is the average expressed in base five?|114|141|401|411": "Step 1: Convert to base ten: 1214_{5} = 1×125 + 2×25 + 1×5 + 4 = 184. Step 2: Average = 184 ÷ 4 = 46. Step 3: Back to base five: 46 = 1×25 + 4×5 + 1, so 141_{5}: B. The trap: dividing the base-five digits directly by 4, or reversing the remainders, gives 114 (A) or 411 (D). Memory tip: never do division in a strange base; convert to base ten first.",
 "|Given U = {Even numbers between 0 and 30}, P = {Multiples of 6 between 0 and 30}, Q = {Multiples of 4 between 0 and 30}, find (P ∪ Q)'.|{2, 10, 14, 22, 26}|{0, 10, 14, 22, 26}|{2, 4, 14, 18, 26}|{0, 2, 6, 22, 26}": "Step 1: List U = {2, 4, 6, …, 28} (even numbers strictly between 0 and 30). Step 2: P = {6, 12, 18, 24}, Q = {4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 28}. Step 3: P ∪ Q = {4, 6, 8, 12, 16, 18, 20, 24, 28}. Step 4: (P ∪ Q)' = members of U not in P ∪ Q = {2, 10, 14, 22, 26}: A. The trap: 0 is not \"between 0 and 30\", so B and D are out at once. Memory tip: a complement can only contain members of U, so check each option against U first.",
 "|x varies directly as the product of u and v and inversely as their sum. If x = 3 when u = 3 and v = 1, what is the value of x if u = 3 and v = 3?|3|4|6|9": "Step 1: Write the law: x = k·uv/(u + v). Step 2: Put x = 3, u = 3, v = 1: 3 = k × 3/4, so k = 4. Step 3: Now u = 3, v = 3: x = 4 × 9/6 = 6: C. The trap: using direct variation only (x = kuv) gives k = 1 and x = 9 (D). Memory tip: \"directly as\" goes on top, \"inversely as\" goes underneath; find k first, then substitute.",
 "|Find the range of values of x satisfying the inequalities 5 + x ≤ 8 and 13 + x ≥ 7.|−3 ≤ x ≤ 3|3 ≤ x ≤ 6|−6 ≤ x ≤ 3|−6 ≤ x ≤ −3": "Step 1: 5 + x ≤ 8 gives x ≤ 3. Step 2: 13 + x ≥ 7 gives x ≥ 7 − 13 = −6. Step 3: Both together: −6 ≤ x ≤ 3: C. The trap: getting a sign wrong in 7 − 13 gives x ≥ 6 and leads to B. Memory tip: solve each inequality separately, then combine them into one double inequality.",
 "|If det[[−x, 2], [4x, 1]] = det[[3, 3x], [4, −5]], find the value of x.|5|2|−2|−5": "Step 1: Determinant of [[a, b], [c, d]] is ad − bc. Step 2: Left: (−x)(1) − (2)(4x) = −x − 8x = −9x. Step 3: Right: (3)(−5) − (3x)(4) = −15 − 12x. Step 4: −9x = −15 − 12x, so 3x = −15 and x = −5: D. The trap: dropping the minus on −5 gives x = 5 (A). Memory tip: determinant = leading diagonal product minus the other diagonal product.",
 "|The graphs of the function y = x² + 4 and a straight line PQ are drawn to solve the equation x² − 3x + 2 = 0. What is the equation of PQ?|y = 3x − 2|y = 3x + 2|y = 3x − 4|y = 3x + 4": "Step 1: The line PQ must make the two graphs meet exactly where x² − 3x + 2 = 0. Step 2: At the meeting points, x² + 4 = y (curve), so subtract: (x² + 4) − y = x² − 3x + 2. Step 3: Solve for y: y = x² + 4 − x² + 3x − 2 = 3x + 2: B. The trap: subtracting the wrong way round gives y = 3x − 2 (A). Memory tip: line = given curve − equation to be solved (in the form … = 0).",
 "|The length a person can jump is inversely proportional to his weight. If a 20 kg person can jump 1.5 m, find the constant of proportionality.|60|30|20|15": "Step 1: Inversely proportional: L = k/W. Step 2: So k = L × W. Step 3: k = 1.5 × 20 = 30: B. The trap: dividing (20 ÷ 1.5 ≈ 13 or 1.5 ÷ 20) belongs to direct variation, and doubling gives 60 (A). Memory tip: for inverse variation the product of the two quantities is the constant.",
 "|Find the value of x and y respectively if 3x − 5y + 5 = 0 and 4x − 7y + 8 = 0.|−5, −4|−4, −5|4, 5|5, 4": "Step 1: Rewrite: 3x − 5y = −5 and 4x − 7y = −8. Step 2: Make x match: ×4 gives 12x − 20y = −20; ×3 gives 12x − 21y = −24. Step 3: Subtract: y = 4. Step 4: 3x = −5 + 20 = 15, so x = 5. Answer (5, 4): D. Check: 4(5) − 7(4) + 8 = 0. The trap: choosing (4, 5) (C) swaps the order. Memory tip: \"x and y respectively\" means x first; always check your pair in both equations.",
 "|Three consecutive terms of a geometric progression are given as n − 2, n and n + 3. Find the common ratio.|¼|½|2/3|3/2": "Step 1: In a GP the middle term squared equals the product of its neighbours: n² = (n − 2)(n + 3). Step 2: n² = n² + n − 6, so n = 6. Step 3: The terms are 4, 6, 9, and r = 6/4 = 3/2: D. The trap: taking the ratio upside down (4/6 = 2/3) gives C. Memory tip: r = later term ÷ earlier term, and b² = ac for consecutive terms a, b, c.",
 "|Triangle OPQ above is the solution of the inequalities|x + 1 ≥ 0, y + x ≤ 0, y − x ≥ 0|y + x ≤ 0, y − x ≥ 0, x − 1 ≥ 0|x − 1 ≤ 0, y − x ≥ 0, y + x ≥ 0|x − 1 ≤ 0, y + x ≥ 0, y − x ≤ 0": "Step 1: The triangle is bounded by x = −1 (left), y = −x through P and y = x through Q. Step 2: Test an inside point, (−0.5, 0). Step 3: x + 1 = 0.5 ≥ 0 ✓; y + x = −0.5 ≤ 0 ✓; y − x = 0.5 ≥ 0 ✓. This is A. The trap: the other options use x − 1, but the line drawn is x + 1 = 0, so B, C and D fail at once. Memory tip: pick a point clearly inside the region and test it in each inequality.",
 "|Factorize completely 4abx − 2axy − 12b²x + 6bxy.|2x(a − 3b)(2b − y)|2x(3b − a)(2b − y)|2x(a − 3b)(y − 2b)|2x(2b − a)(3b − y)": "Step 1: Common factor 2x: 2x(2ab − ay − 6b² + 3by). Step 2: Group in pairs: a(2b − y) − 3b(2b − y). Step 3: Take out (2b − y): 2x(a − 3b)(2b − y): A. The trap: B and C each flip the sign of only one bracket, which changes the sign of the whole expression. Memory tip: after grouping, the brackets must be identical; check a sign change by expanding one term.",
 "|A matrix P has an inverse P^{−1} = [[1, −3], [0, 1]]. Find P.|[[−1, 3], [0, −1]]|[[1, 3], [0, −1]]|[[1, −3], [0, −1]]|[[1, 3], [0, 1]]": "Step 1: The inverse of the inverse is the matrix itself, so P = (P^{−1})^{−1}. Step 2: For [[1, −3], [0, 1]], det = 1×1 − (−3)(0) = 1. Step 3: Swap the leading diagonal and change the signs of the other two: [[1, 3], [0, 1]], divided by det 1: D. Check: [[1, 3], [0, 1]] × [[1, −3], [0, 1]] = I. The trap: changing signs on the diagonal too gives B or C. Memory tip: for a 2×2 inverse, swap a and d, negate b and c, divide by the determinant.",
 "|The sum of the first n terms of an arithmetic progression is 252. If the first term is −16 and the last term is 72, find the number of terms in the series.|6|7|8|9": "Step 1: With first and last terms known, S_{n} = n(a + l)/2. Step 2: 252 = n(−16 + 72)/2 = 28n. Step 3: n = 252/28 = 9: D. The trap: using a + l = 88 (ignoring the minus) gives about 5.7, which leads to guessing 6 (A). Memory tip: S_{n} = n/2 × (first + last) saves finding d.",
 "|An arc of a circle subtends an angle of 30° on the circumference of a circle of radius 21 cm. Find the length of the arc. [π = 22/7]|11 cm|22 cm|44 cm|66 cm": "Step 1: The angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference: 2 × 30° = 60°. Step 2: Arc length = θ/360 × 2πr = 60/360 × 2 × 22/7 × 21. Step 3: = 1/6 × 132 = 22 cm: B. The trap: using 30° as the centre angle gives 11 cm (A). Memory tip: arc-length formulas need the angle at the CENTRE.",
 "|Find the equation of the locus of a point P(x, y) which is equidistant from Q(0, 0) and R(2, 1).|4x + 2y = 5|4x − 2y = 5|2x + 2y = 5|2x + y = 5": "Step 1: Equidistant means PQ² = PR²: x² + y² = (x − 2)² + (y − 1)². Step 2: Expand: x² + y² = x² − 4x + 4 + y² − 2y + 1. Step 3: Cancel: 0 = −4x − 2y + 5, so 4x + 2y = 5: A. The trap: a sign slip in expanding (y − 1)² gives 4x − 2y = 5 (B). Memory tip: the locus of points equidistant from two points is their perpendicular bisector; it passes through the midpoint (1, ½), and 4(1) + 2(½) = 5 checks.",
 "|In the diagram above, PQ is parallel to RS. What is the value of α + β + γ?|360°|200°|180°|90°": "Step 1: Draw a line through the vertex of β parallel to PQ and RS. Step 2: It splits β into β₁ (next to α) and β₂ (next to γ). Step 3: α and β₁ are co-interior angles between parallels, so α + β₁ = 180°; likewise γ + β₂ = 180°. Step 4: α + β + γ = 360°: A. The trap: thinking of alternate angles (Z-shape) gives 180° (C), but here the angles face each other inside, so they are co-interior. Memory tip: co-interior (U-shape) angles add to 180°; add an extra parallel line through any bend.",
 "|A trapezium has two parallel sides of length 5 cm and 9 cm. If the area is 21 cm², find the distance between the parallel sides.|3 cm|4 cm|6 cm|7 cm": "Step 1: Area of a trapezium = ½(sum of parallel sides) × height. Step 2: 21 = ½(5 + 9) × h = 7h. Step 3: h = 3 cm: A. The trap: forgetting the ½ gives 14h = 21 and h = 1.5, which is not offered; dividing 21 by 5 or 9 also fails. (The paper misprints \"21 cm²\" as \"21st cm²\".) Memory tip: trapezium area = average of the parallel sides × the distance between them.",
 "|Which of the following is the graph of sin θ for −π/2 ≤ θ ≤ 3π/2?|Graph A|Graph B|Graph C|Graph D": "Step 1: Plot key values: sin(−π/2) = −1, sin 0 = 0, sin(π/2) = 1, sin π = 0, sin(3π/2) = −1. Step 2: So the curve starts at −1, rises through the origin, peaks at π/2, comes back to 0 at π and falls to −1 at 3π/2. Step 3: Only graph D rises through the origin to a peak at π/2: D. The key gives B, but graph B is −1 at θ = 0 and peaks at π, which is −cos θ, not sin θ. A is −sin θ and C is cos θ. Memory tip: sin starts at 0 going up; cos starts at its maximum 1.",
 "|In the diagram above, O is the centre of the circle. POM is a diameter and ∠MNQ = 42°. Calculate ∠QMP.|42°|48°|132°|138°": "Step 1: ∠MPQ and ∠MNQ stand on the same arc MQ, so ∠MPQ = 42°. Step 2: MP is a diameter, so ∠MQP = 90° (angle in a semicircle). Step 3: In triangle MQP: ∠QMP = 180° − 90° − 42° = 48°: B. The trap: copying the 42° straight across gives A. Memory tip: whenever a diameter appears, look for a 90° angle at the circumference.",
 "|Find the value of p, if the line which passes through (−1, −p) and (−2p, 2) is parallel to the line 2y + 8x − 17 = 0.|7/6|6/7|−2/7|−6/7": "Step 1: Gradient of 2y + 8x − 17 = 0: y = −4x + 17/2, so m = −4. Step 2: Parallel lines have equal gradients: (2 − (−p))/(−2p − (−1)) = (2 + p)/(1 − 2p) = −4. Step 3: 2 + p = −4 + 8p, so 7p = 6 and p = 6/7: B. The trap: a sign slip with −(−p) gives −6/7 (D). Memory tip: make y the subject to read off the gradient; parallel means equal gradients.",
 "|An aeroplane flies due north from airport P to Q and then flies due east to R. If Q is equidistant from P and R, find the bearing of P from R.|090°|135°|225°|270°": "Step 1: Let PQ = QR = d. With P at (0, 0), Q is (0, d) and R is (d, d). Step 2: From R, P is d units south and d units west, so the direction is exactly south-west. Step 3: South-west is 180° + 45° = 225°: C. The trap: 045° is the bearing of R from P; and 135° (B) is south-east. Memory tip: back bearing = forward bearing ± 180°: 045° + 180° = 225°.",
 "|The locus of a point P which moves on one side only of a straight line XY so that ∠XPY = 90° is|a circle|a semicircle|an arc of a circle through X, Y|the perpendicular bisector of XY": "Step 1: The angle in a semicircle is 90°, so every point P with ∠XPY = 90° lies on the circle with XY as diameter. Step 2: The question restricts P to one side of XY. Step 3: So P traces half of that circle, a semicircle: B. The trap: \"a circle\" (A) would put points on both sides of XY. Memory tip: a fixed 90° angle on a fixed segment means a circle on that segment as diameter.",
 "|If π/2 ≤ θ ≤ 2π, find the maximum value of f(θ) = 4/(6 + 2 cos θ).|4|1|2/3|½": "Step 1: f(θ) = 4/(6 + 2 cos θ) is largest when the denominator is smallest. Step 2: cos θ is smallest (−1) at θ = π, which lies in π/2 ≤ θ ≤ 2π. Step 3: f_{max} = 4/(6 − 2) = 4/4 = 1: B. The trap: using cos θ = 1 gives the minimum, 4/8 = ½ (D). (The paper prints \"cos π\" for \"cos θ\"; cos π = −1 gives 1 as well.) Memory tip: to maximise a fraction with a fixed top, minimise the bottom.",
 "|XYZ is a circle centre O and radius 7 cm. Find the area of the shaded region.|84 cm²|77 cm²|38 cm²|14 cm²": "Step 1: ∠XZY = 45° at the circumference, so ∠XOY = 90° at the centre. Step 2: Sector XOY = 90/360 × 22/7 × 7² = 38.5 cm². Step 3: Triangle XOY = ½ × 7 × 7 × sin 90° = 24.5 cm². Step 4: Shaded segment = 38.5 − 24.5 = 14 cm²: D. The trap: stopping at the sector gives about 38 (C). Memory tip: segment area = sector − triangle.",
 "|A triangle has vertices P(−1, 6), Q(−3, −4) and R(1, −4). Find the midpoints of PQ and QR respectively.|(0, −2) and (−1, −4)|(−1, 0) and (−1, −1)|(−2, 1) and (0, 1)|(−2, 1) and (−1, −4)": "Step 1: Midpoint = ((x₁ + x₂)/2, (y₁ + y₂)/2). Step 2: PQ: ((−1 + (−3))/2, (6 + (−4))/2) = (−2, 1). Step 3: QR: ((−3 + 1)/2, (−4 + (−4))/2) = (−1, −4). Answer: D. The trap: C has the right first point but finds the midpoint of PR, (0, 1), for the second. Memory tip: midpoint = average the x's, average the y's; check which pair of points you used.",
 "|In the diagram above, PQR is a straight line and PS is a tangent to the circle QRS with |PS| = |SR| and ∠SPR = 40°. Find ∠PSQ.|40°|30°|20°|10°": "Step 1: |PS| = |SR|, so triangle PSR is isosceles and ∠SRP = ∠SPR = 40°. Step 2: ∠SRQ is the same angle (Q lies on PR), so ∠SRQ = 40°. Step 3: Alternate segment theorem: the angle between tangent PS and chord SQ equals the angle in the opposite segment, ∠SRQ. So ∠PSQ = 40°: A. The trap: halving the angle gives 20° (C). Memory tip: tangent–chord angle = angle in the alternate segment.",
 "|Find the slope of the curve y = 2x² + 5x − 3 at (1, 4).|4|6|7|9": "Step 1: The slope of a curve is dy/dx. Step 2: y = 2x² + 5x − 3 gives dy/dx = 4x + 5. Step 3: At x = 1: 4 + 5 = 9: D. The trap: substituting x = 1 into y gives 4 (A), which is the y-value, not the slope. Memory tip: slope at a point = derivative evaluated at that x.",
 "|Evaluate ∫_{2}^{3} (x² − 2x) dx.|4|2|4/3|1/3": "Step 1: Integrate: ∫(x² − 2x) dx = x³/3 − x². Step 2: At 3: 27/3 − 9 = 0. Step 3: At 2: 8/3 − 4 = −4/3. Step 4: 0 − (−4/3) = 4/3: C. The trap: subtracting the wrong way (lower − upper) gives −4/3, and slips with the minus give 4 (A). Memory tip: ∫_{a}^{b} f dx = F(b) − F(a), upper limit first.",
 "|If y = 3 sin(−4x), dy/dx is|12x cos(4x)|−12x cos(−4x)|−12 cos(−4x)|−12 sin(−4x)": "Step 1: Chain rule: d/dx sin(u) = cos(u) × du/dx with u = −4x. Step 2: du/dx = −4. Step 3: dy/dx = 3 × cos(−4x) × (−4) = −12 cos(−4x): C. The trap: A and B wrongly keep an x outside the cosine, and D forgets that sin differentiates to cos. Memory tip: d/dx sin(ax) = a cos(ax).",
 "|Determine the maximum value of y = 3x² − x³.|0|2|4|6": "Step 1: dy/dx = 6x − 3x² = 3x(2 − x) = 0, so x = 0 or x = 2. Step 2: d²y/dx² = 6 − 6x: at x = 0 it is +6 (minimum), at x = 2 it is −6 (maximum). Step 3: y(2) = 3(4) − 8 = 4: C. The trap: choosing 0 (A) is the minimum value, and 2 (B) is the x-value, not y. Memory tip: the maximum VALUE is y, not x; use the second derivative to tell max from min.",
 "|By how much is the mean of 30, 56, 31, 55, 43 and 44 less than the median?|0.75|0.50|0.33|0.17": "Step 1: Mean = (30 + 56 + 31 + 55 + 43 + 44)/6 = 259/6 = 43.17. Step 2: Arrange: 30, 31, 43, 44, 55, 56. Median = (43 + 44)/2 = 43.5. Step 3: 43.5 − 43.17 = 0.33: C. The trap: taking the median as 43 or 44 without averaging gives other differences. Memory tip: for an even count, the median is the average of the two middle values.",
 "|The range of 4, 3, 11, 9, 6, 15, 19, 23, 27, 24, 21 and 16 is|16|21|23|24": "Step 1: Find the largest value: 27. Step 2: Find the smallest value: 3. Step 3: Range = 27 − 3 = 24: D. The trap: subtracting the first and last numbers in the list (16 − 4 = 12) or missing the 3 gives other values, such as 23 (C) from 27 − 4. Memory tip: range = highest − lowest; scan the whole list, not just its ends.",
 "|Number: 0, 1, 2, 3, 4. Frequency: 1, 2, 2, 1, 9. Find the mean of the distribution above.|1|2|3|4": "Step 1: Multiply each number by its frequency: 0×1 + 1×2 + 2×2 + 3×1 + 4×9 = 0 + 2 + 4 + 3 + 36 = 45. Step 2: Total frequency = 1 + 2 + 2 + 1 + 9 = 15. Step 3: Mean = 45/15 = 3: C. The trap: averaging the numbers 0–4 alone gives 2 (B). Memory tip: mean of a frequency table = Σfx ÷ Σf.",
 "|On a pie chart, there are four sectors of which three angles are 45°, 90° and 135°. If the smallest sector represents ₦28.00, how much is the largest sector?|₦84.00|₦42.00|₦48.00|₦96.00": "Step 1: Fourth angle = 360° − (45° + 90° + 135°) = 90°. Step 2: Smallest is 45° = ₦28, largest is 135°. Step 3: 135° is 3 times 45°, so ₦28 × 3 = ₦84: A. The trap: using 90° as the largest gives ₦56, and adding instead of scaling gives the other options. Memory tip: in a pie chart, amounts are proportional to angles.",
 "|Two dice are thrown. What is the probability that the sum of the numbers is divisible by 3?|2/3|½|1/3|¼": "Step 1: There are 6 × 6 = 36 outcomes. Step 2: Sums divisible by 3 are 3, 6, 9, 12. Step 3: Count: sum 3 → 2 ways, 6 → 5 ways, 9 → 4 ways, 12 → 1 way; total 12. Step 4: P = 12/36 = 1/3: C. The trap: listing only the sums (4 out of 11 possible totals) ignores that some sums are more likely. Memory tip: with two dice, count ordered pairs out of 36.",
 "|If ^{n}P_{3} − 6(^{n}C_{4}) = 0, find the value of n.|5|6|7|8": "Step 1: ^{n}P_{3} = n(n − 1)(n − 2) and ^{n}C_{4} = n(n − 1)(n − 2)(n − 3)/24. Step 2: n(n − 1)(n − 2) = 6 × n(n − 1)(n − 2)(n − 3)/24. Step 3: Divide both sides by n(n − 1)(n − 2): 1 = (n − 3)/4, so n = 7: C. Check: ^{7}P_{3} = 210 and 6 × ^{7}C_{4} = 6 × 35 = 210. Memory tip: write P and C as products and cancel the common factors.",
 "|Find the number of committees of three that can be formed consisting of two men and one woman from four men and three women.|3|6|18|24": "Step 1: Choose 2 men from 4: ^{4}C_{2} = 6. Step 2: Choose 1 woman from 3: ^{3}C_{1} = 3. Step 3: Multiply the independent choices: 6 × 3 = 18: C. The trap: adding (6 + 3 = 9) or using arrangements, 4 × 3 × 2 = 24 (D), counts wrongly; a committee has no order. Memory tip: \"and\" between choices means multiply; committees use combinations.",
 "|The mean of the numbers 3, 6, 4, x and 7 is 5. Find the standard deviation.|√2|√3|2|3": "Step 1: Sum = 5 × 5 = 25, so x = 25 − (3 + 6 + 4 + 7) = 5. Step 2: Deviations from the mean 5: −2, 1, −1, 0, 2. Step 3: Squares: 4, 1, 1, 0, 4; sum 10. Step 4: Variance = 10/5 = 2, SD = √2: A. The trap: forgetting the square root gives 2 (C). Memory tip: SD = √(Σ(x − x̄)²/n); variance is before the square root.",
 "|A bag contains 5 black balls and 3 red balls. Two balls are picked at random without replacement. What is the probability that a black and a red ball are picked?|15/28|13/28|5/14|3/14": "Step 1: \"A black and a red\" can happen in two orders. Step 2: Black then red: 5/8 × 3/7 = 15/56. Step 3: Red then black: 3/8 × 5/7 = 15/56. Step 4: Total = 30/56 = 15/28: A. The trap: counting only one order gives 15/56, and simplifying that wrongly leads to the other fractions. Memory tip: for \"one of each\" without replacement, add both orders (or use ^{5}C_{1} × ^{3}C_{1} / ^{8}C_{2} = 15/28).",
 "|The histogram above shows the ages of the victims of a pollution. How many people were involved in the pollution?|15|18|20|21": "Step 1: Read each bar's height on the \"No. of people\" axis. Step 2: 0–10: 3, 10–20: 4, 20–30: 5, 30–40: 6, 40–50: 2. Step 3: Total = 3 + 4 + 5 + 6 + 2 = 20: C. The trap: leaving out the short last bar gives 18 (B). Memory tip: when the class widths are equal, the number of people is the sum of the bar heights.",
 "|Number: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6. Frequency: 12, 20, x, 21, x − 1, 28. The result of tossing a fair die 120 times is summarized above. Find the value of x.|19|20|21|22": "Step 1: The frequencies must add up to the number of tosses, 120. Step 2: 12 + 20 + x + 21 + (x − 1) + 28 = 120, so 2x + 80 = 120. Step 3: 2x = 40 and x = 20: B. The trap: forgetting the −1 gives 2x + 81 = 120 and x = 19.5, so some students choose 19 (A). Memory tip: in a frequency table, Σf = the total number of trials.",
 "|An arc of a circle of length 22 cm subtends an angle of 3x° at the centre of the circle. Find the value of x if the diameter of the circle is 14 cm. [Take π = 22/7]|60°|120°|180°|30°": "Step 1: Radius = 14 ÷ 2 = 7 cm, so circumference = 2 × 22/7 × 7 = 44 cm. Step 2: Arc length = (θ/360°) × circumference, so 22 = (θ/360°) × 44, giving θ = 180°. Step 3: The angle is 3x°, so 3x = 180 and x = 60°: A. The trap: stopping at θ = 180° picks C, but the question asks for x, not the whole angle. Memory tip: arc/circumference = angle/360°; always finish by answering for the letter the question names.",
 "|Find the value of α² + β² if α + β = 2 and the distance between the points (1, α) and (β, 1) is 3 units.|14|3|5|11": "Step 1: Distance squared: (β − 1)² + (1 − α)² = 3² = 9. Step 2: Expand: β² − 2β + 1 + 1 − 2α + α² = 9, i.e. α² + β² − 2(α + β) + 2 = 9. Step 3: Put α + β = 2: α² + β² − 4 + 2 = 9, so α² + β² = 11: D. The trap: forgetting to square the distance (using 3 instead of 9) gives 5 (C). Memory tip: when a question gives a sum like α + β, expand and look for that sum instead of finding α and β separately.",
 "|The sum of the interior angles of a pentagon is 6x + 6y. Find y in terms of x.|y = 90 − x|y = 150 − x|y = 60 − x|y = 120 − x": "Step 1: Sum of interior angles of an n-sided polygon = (n − 2) × 180°. Step 2: For a pentagon, n = 5: 3 × 180° = 540°. Step 3: 6x + 6y = 540, divide by 6: x + y = 90, so y = 90 − x: A. The trap: using 900° or 720° (the wrong number of sides) gives 150 − x or 120 − x. Memory tip: (n − 2) × 180° for the angle sum; a pentagon is 540°, a hexagon 720°.",
 "|In the diagram, PQ = 4 cm and TS = 6 cm. If the area of parallelogram PQTU is 32 cm², find the area of the trapezium PQRU. (U is the unlabelled bottom-left corner.)|60 cm²|72 cm²|48 cm²|24 cm²": "Step 1: In parallelogram PQTU, opposite sides are equal, so UT = PQ = 4 cm. Likewise PQRS is a parallelogram, so SR = 4 cm. Step 2: Base UR = UT + TS + SR = 4 + 6 + 4 = 14 cm. Step 3: Height: area of PQTU = base × height, so 32 = 4 × h, h = 8 cm. Step 4: Trapezium area = ½(4 + 14) × 8 = 72 cm²: B. The trap: using UR = 4 + 6 = 10 cm forgets SR and gives 56, not an option, or using TS alone leads to 40. Memory tip: trapezium area = ½(sum of parallel sides) × height.",
 "|Find the midpoint of the line joining P(−3, 5) and Q(5, −3).|(1, 1)|(2, 2)|(4, 4)|(4, −4)": "Step 1: Midpoint = ((x₁ + x₂)/2, (y₁ + y₂)/2). Step 2: x: (−3 + 5)/2 = 2/2 = 1. Step 3: y: (5 + (−3))/2 = 2/2 = 1. So the midpoint is (1, 1): A. The trap: forgetting to divide by 2 gives (2, 2) (B); subtracting instead of adding gives (4, −4) (D), which is half the change, not the midpoint. Memory tip: midpoint = average of the x's, average of the y's.",
 "|PQRSTV is a regular polygon of side 7 cm inscribed in a circle. Find the circumference of the circle PQRSTV. [π = 22/7]|56 cm|22 cm|42 cm|44 cm": "Step 1: PQRSTV has six vertices, so it is a regular hexagon. Step 2: A regular hexagon is made of six equilateral triangles meeting at the centre, so the radius equals the side: r = 7 cm. Step 3: Circumference = 2πr = 2 × 22/7 × 7 = 44 cm: D. The trap: using 7 cm as the diameter gives 22 cm (B); using the perimeter 6 × 7 = 42 cm (C) confuses the hexagon with the circle. Memory tip: in a regular hexagon, side = radius of the circumscribed circle.",
 "|Determine the locus of a point inside a square PQRS which is equidistant from PQ and QR.|The diagonal QS|The perpendicular bisector of PQ|The diagonal PR|Side SR": "Step 1: The locus of points equidistant from two lines that meet is the bisector of the angle between them. Step 2: PQ and QR meet at corner Q of the square, making a 90° angle. Step 3: The diagonal QS cuts the right angle at Q into two 45° angles, so it is that bisector: A. The trap: the diagonal PR (C) goes through P and R, not Q; the perpendicular bisector of PQ (B) is equidistant from the points P and Q, not the lines. Memory tip: equidistant from two points → perpendicular bisector; from two lines → angle bisector.",
 "|Find the value of x in the figure.|15√6|20√6|3√6|5√6": "Step 1: Name the sides by the angles opposite them. The 15 cm side is opposite the 60° angle; x is opposite the 45° angle. Step 2: Sine rule: x/sin 45° = 15/sin 60°. Step 3: x = 15 × (√2/2) ÷ (√3/2) = 15√2/√3. Step 4: Rationalise: 15√2 × √3/3 = 15√6/3 = 5√6: D. The trap: pairing 15 with the 45° angle (the angle next to it, not opposite) gives x = 15√3/√2 = 15√6/2, which is not offered. Memory tip: in the sine rule each side pairs with the angle facing it.",
 "|P, R and S lie on a circle centre O as shown, while Q lies outside the circle. Find ∠PSO.|45°|55°|35°|40°": "Step 1: In △PQR, ∠Q = 35° and ∠QPR = 20°, so the exterior angle at R is ∠PRS = 35° + 20° = 55°. Step 2: ∠PRS is an angle at the circumference standing on arc PS, so the angle at the centre is ∠POS = 2 × 55° = 110°. Step 3: OP = OS (radii), so △POS is isosceles: ∠PSO = (180° − 110°)/2 = 35°: C. The trap: stopping at 55° gives B. Memory tip: exterior angle of a triangle = sum of the two opposite interior angles; angle at centre = 2 × angle at circumference.",
 "|The shadow of a pole 5√3 m high is 5 m. Find the angle of elevation of the sun.|45°|75°|30°|60°": "Step 1: The paper prints \"pole 5   high\" with a gap: the height should read 5√3 m, which a printing slip lost. Step 2: tan θ = opposite/adjacent = height/shadow = 5√3/5 = √3. Step 3: tan θ = √3 gives θ = 60°: D, the key's answer. The trap: reading the height as just 5 m gives tan θ = 1 and 45° (A); that is the answer only to the misprinted wording. Memory tip: tan 30° = 1/√3, tan 45° = 1, tan 60° = √3; for the sun, angle of elevation = tan^{−1}(height ÷ shadow).",
 "|The locus of a point which is 5 cm from the line LM is a|line distance 10 cm from LM and parallel to LM|pair of lines on opposite sides of LM and parallel to it, each distance 5 cm from LM|line parallel to LM and 5 cm from LM|pair of parallel lines on one side of LM and parallel to LM": "Step 1: A point 5 cm from line LM can be on either side of LM. Step 2: On each side, all such points form a line parallel to LM at distance 5 cm. Step 3: So the locus is a pair of parallel lines, one on each side of LM, each 5 cm from it: B. The trap: C forgets the second side; A doubles the distance to 10 cm; D puts both lines on one side. Memory tip: locus at a fixed distance from a line = two parallel lines; from a point = a circle.",
 "|If y = 3 cos (x/3), find dy/dx when x = 3π/2.|1|−3|2|−1": "Step 1: Chain rule: d/dx [cos (x/3)] = −sin (x/3) × 1/3. Step 2: dy/dx = 3 × (−1/3) sin (x/3) = −sin (x/3). Step 3: At x = 3π/2, x/3 = π/2, and sin (π/2) = 1, so dy/dx = −1: D. The trap: forgetting the factor 1/3 gives −3 (B); forgetting the minus sign gives 1 (A). Memory tip: d/dx cos (ax) = −a sin (ax); d/dx sin (ax) = a cos (ax).",
 "|Find the derivative of (2 + 3x)(1 − x) with respect to x.|6|−3|1 − 6x|6x − 1": "Step 1: Expand first: (2 + 3x)(1 − x) = 2 − 2x + 3x − 3x² = 2 + x − 3x². Step 2: Differentiate term by term: 0 + 1 − 6x. So the derivative is 1 − 6x: C. The trap: multiplying the derivatives of the brackets, 3 × (−1) = −3 (B), is wrong; the product rule (or expanding) is needed. Memory tip: d/dx (uv) = u dv/dx + v du/dx; for short brackets, expanding first is quickest.",
 "|What is the rate of change of the volume v of a hemisphere with respect to its radius r when r = 2?|8π|16π|2π|4π": "Step 1: Volume of a hemisphere = half a sphere: v = ½ × (4/3)πr³ = (2/3)πr³. Step 2: dv/dr = 3 × (2/3)πr² = 2πr². Step 3: At r = 2: 2π × 4 = 8π: A. The trap: using the full sphere, dv/dr = 4πr² = 16π, gives B. Memory tip: sphere V = (4/3)πr³ and dV/dr = 4πr² (the surface area); halve both for a hemisphere.",
 "|Find the derivative of the function y = 2x²(2x − 1) at the point x = −1.|18|16|−4|−6": "Step 1: Expand: y = 2x²(2x − 1) = 4x³ − 2x². Step 2: dy/dx = 12x² − 4x. Step 3: At x = −1: 12(1) − 4(−1) = 12 + 4 = 16: B. The trap: a sign slip with −4x at x = −1 gives 12 − 4 = 8, or substituting into y instead of dy/dx gives −6 (D). Memory tip: expand, differentiate, then substitute, and bracket negative values when you substitute.",
 "|Evaluate ∫_{1}^{3} (x² − 1) dx.|2/3|−2/3|−6 2/3|6 2/3": "Step 1: Integrate: ∫(x² − 1) dx = x³/3 − x. Step 2: Upper limit 3: 27/3 − 3 = 6. Lower limit 1: 1/3 − 1 = −2/3. Step 3: Subtract: 6 − (−2/3) = 6 + 2/3 = 6 2/3: D. The trap: subtracting the wrong way round gives −6 2/3 (C); mishandling the minus of −2/3 gives 5 1/3. Memory tip: definite integral = F(upper) − F(lower); bracket the lower value before subtracting.",
 "|Some white balls were put in a basket containing twelve red balls and sixteen black balls. If the probability of picking a white ball from the basket is 3/7, how many white balls were introduced?|12|21|28|32": "Step 1: Let w be the number of white balls. Total = w + 12 + 16 = w + 28. Step 2: P(white) = w/(w + 28) = 3/7. Step 3: Cross-multiply: 7w = 3w + 84, so 4w = 84 and w = 21: B. Check: 21/49 = 3/7. The trap: using 3/7 of the original 28 gives 12 (A), forgetting that the white balls are part of the total. Memory tip: probability = favourable ÷ total, and the total must include the items just added.",
 "|Find the mean deviation of 1, 2, 3 and 4.|2.5|2.0|1.0|1.5": "Step 1: Read the frequency of 5 from the table: 20. Step 2: The die was rolled 100 times (13 + 15 + 22 + 14 + 20 + 16 = 100). Step 3: Experimental probability = 20/100 = 1/5: A. The trap: the theoretical probability for a fair die is 1/6 (C), but the question asks about these recorded results. Memory tip: experimental probability = number of times the event happened ÷ number of trials.",
 "|An unbiased die is rolled 100 times and the outcome is tabulated as follows: Number 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 with frequencies 13, 15, 22, 14, 20, 16 respectively. What is the probability of obtaining 5?|1/5|1/2|1/6|1/4": "Step 1: Mean = (1 + 2 + 3 + 4)/4 = 2.5. Step 2: Absolute deviations from the mean: |1 − 2.5| = 1.5, |2 − 2.5| = 0.5, |3 − 2.5| = 0.5, |4 − 2.5| = 1.5. Step 3: Mean deviation = (1.5 + 0.5 + 0.5 + 1.5)/4 = 4/4 = 1.0: C. The trap: giving the mean itself, 2.5 (A), or forgetting the absolute value (the signed deviations add to 0). Memory tip: mean deviation = Σ|x − x̄| ÷ n.",
 "|The pie chart shows the distribution of the crops harvested from a farmland in a year. If 3000 tonnes of millet is harvested, what amount of beans is harvested?|6000 tonnes|1500 tonnes|1200 tonnes|9000 tonnes": "Step 1: 150° of millet = 3000 t, so 1° = 3000 ÷ 150 = 20 t. Step 2: The chart prints only Others 60° and Millet 150°; the maize angle is marked as a right angle but its size is not printed. Taking maize = 90°: beans = 360° − 150° − 60° − 90° = 60°. Step 3: Beans = 60 × 20 = 1200 t: C, the key's answer. The trap: sharing the remaining 150° equally gives 75° and 1500 t (B). Memory tip: find the quantity per degree first, then multiply by the sector's angle.",
 "|In how many ways can 2 students be selected from a group of 5 students in a debating competition?|25 ways|10 ways|15 ways|20 ways": "Step 1: Choosing 2 students out of 5 where order does not matter is a combination. Step 2: ⁵C₂ = 5!/(2! × 3!) = (5 × 4)/(2 × 1) = 10 ways: B. The trap: ⁵P₂ = 5 × 4 = 20 (D) counts each pair twice (AB and BA); 5² = 25 (A) allows the same student twice. Memory tip: selecting a group → combination ⁿCᵣ; arranging in order → permutation ⁿPᵣ.",
 "|The mean age of a group of students is 15 years. When the age of a teacher, 45 years old, is added to the ages of the students, the mean of their ages becomes 18 years. Find the number of students in the group.|9|7|42|15": "Step 1: Let n be the number of students. Their total age = 15n. Step 2: Adding the teacher: total = 15n + 45 for n + 1 people, and the new mean is 18: (15n + 45)/(n + 1) = 18. Step 3: 15n + 45 = 18n + 18, so 27 = 3n and n = 9: A. Check: 9 × 15 + 45 = 180 = 10 × 18. The trap: forgetting that the group grows to n + 1 gives 45/3 = 15 (D). Memory tip: total = mean × number; write the new total over the new number.",
 "|A container has 30 gold medals, 22 silver medals and 18 bronze medals. If one medal is selected at random from the container, what is the probability that it is not a gold medal?|9/35|11/35|4/7|3/7": "Step 1: Total medals = 30 + 22 + 18 = 70. Step 2: Not gold = silver + bronze = 22 + 18 = 40. Step 3: P(not gold) = 40/70 = 4/7: C. Check: P(gold) = 30/70 = 3/7 and 1 − 3/7 = 4/7. The trap: P(gold) = 3/7 (D) answers the opposite question. Memory tip: P(not A) = 1 − P(A).",
 "|A committee of six is to be formed by a state governor from nine state commissioners and three members of the state house of assembly. In how many ways can the members of the committee be chosen so as to include one member of the house of assembly?|378 ways|462 ways|840 ways|924 ways": "Step 1: \"Include one member of the house of assembly\" means exactly one of the 3 assembly members. Step 2: Ways to choose that one: ³C₁ = 3. Step 3: The other 5 places come from the 9 commissioners: ⁹C₅ = 126. Step 4: Multiply: 3 × 126 = 378: A. The trap: ignoring the condition and choosing any 6 from 12 gives ¹²C₆ = 924 (D); ¹²C₅ = 792 is not offered. Memory tip: for \"exactly k from group A\", multiply ᴬC_k by the choices for the remaining places from the other group.",
 "|The weights of 10 pupils in a class are 15 kg, 16 kg, 17 kg, 18 kg, 16 kg, 17 kg, 17 kg, 18 kg and 16 kg. What is the range of this distribution?|4|3|1|2": "Step 1: Find the largest and smallest values in the list: highest 18 kg, lowest 15 kg. Step 2: Range = 18 − 15 = 3: B. (Only nine weights are printed for \"10 pupils\", but that does not change the range.) The trap: taking the range of the repeated values 16 to 18 gives 2 (D); confusing range with the number of distinct weights gives 4 (A). Memory tip: range = largest − smallest; it ignores everything in between.",
 "|I. Rectangular bars of equal width; II. The height of each rectangular bar is proportional to the frequency of the corresponding class interval; III. Rectangular bars have common sides with no gaps in between. A histogram is described completely by|I, II and III|I and II|II and III|I and III": "Step 1: Statement I: with equal class intervals the bars have equal widths. Step 2: Statement II: the height of each bar shows the frequency of its class. Step 3: Statement III: the bars stand side by side with no gaps, because the classes are continuous. All three describe the histogram, so the answer is I, II and III: A. The trap: no gaps (III) is the feature that separates a histogram from a bar chart, so any option without III is incomplete. Memory tip: bar chart → gaps between bars; histogram → no gaps, bars touching.",
 "|y is inversely proportional to x and y = 4 when x = ½. Find x when y = 10.|2|10|1/5|1/10": "Step 1: Inverse proportion: y = k/x, so k = xy. Step 2: k = ½ × 4 = 2. Step 3: When y = 10: x = k/y = 2/10 = 1/5: C. The trap: treating it as direct proportion (y = kx, k = 8) gives x = 10/8, not an option, and 1/10 (D) comes from x = 1/y, forgetting the constant k = 2. Memory tip: inversely proportional means the product xy stays constant.",
 "|What are the integral values of x which satisfy the inequality −1 < 3 − 2x ≤ 5?|−1, 0, 1, 2|−2, 1, 0, −1|0, 1, 2|−1, 0, 1": "Step 1: Subtract 3 throughout: −1 − 3 < −2x ≤ 5 − 3, so −4 < −2x ≤ 2. Step 2: Divide by −2 and reverse both signs: 2 > x ≥ −1, i.e. −1 ≤ x < 2. Step 3: Integers: −1, 0, 1 (2 is excluded by the strict sign): D. The trap: forgetting to reverse the signs, or treating \"< 2\" as \"≤ 2\", gives −1, 0, 1, 2 (A). Memory tip: multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number reverses the sign.",
 "|The inverse of the matrix [[2, 1], [1, 1]] is|[[1, 1], [1, 2]]|[[1, 1], [−1, 2]]|[[1, −1], [−1, 2]]|[[1, −1], [−1, 2]]": "Step 1: For [[a, b], [c, d]], the inverse is (1/det) × [[d, −b], [−c, a]]. Step 2: det = 2 × 1 − 1 × 1 = 1. Step 3: Swap 2 and 1 on the leading diagonal and change the signs of the others: [[1, −1], [−1, 2]]: D. Options C and D are printed exactly the same, so C is also accepted alongside the key's D. The trap: forgetting to change the signs gives [[1, 1], [1, 2]]-type answers (A, B). Memory tip: swap the leading diagonal, negate the other diagonal, divide by the determinant.",
 "|Find the values of x where the curve y = x³ + 2x² − 5x − 6 crosses the x-axis.|−2, 1 and −3|2, −1 and −3|2, 1 and 3|−2, −1 and 3": "Step 1: The curve crosses the x-axis where y = 0: x³ + 2x² − 5x − 6 = 0. Step 2: Try x = −1: −1 + 2 + 5 − 6 = 0, so (x + 1) is a factor. Step 3: Divide: x³ + 2x² − 5x − 6 = (x + 1)(x² + x − 6) = (x + 1)(x − 2)(x + 3). Step 4: Roots: x = −1, 2, −3: B. The trap: reading the factors (x + 1), (x − 2), (x + 3) as roots +1, −2, +3 flips every sign. Memory tip: test small divisors of the constant term (±1, ±2, ±3, ±6) to find a first factor.",
 "|Given that the first and fourth terms of a G.P. are 6 and 162 respectively, find the sum of the first three terms of the progression.|27|8|78|48": "Step 1: G.P.: T₁ = a = 6 and T₄ = ar³ = 162. Step 2: r³ = 162/6 = 27, so r = 3. Step 3: First three terms: 6, 18, 54. Sum = 78: C. Check with S₃ = a(r³ − 1)/(r − 1) = 6 × 26/2 = 78. The trap: treating it as an A.P. (common difference 52) gives 6 + 58 + 110, not offered; adding only two terms gives 24. Memory tip: in a G.P., Tₙ = arⁿ⁻¹, so dividing two terms isolates a power of r.",
 "|The shaded area in the diagram is represented by (the boundary line passes through (0, 6) and (2, 0))|{(x, y): y − 3 < −6}|{(x, y): y + 3x < 6}|{(x, y): y + 3x < −6}|{(x, y): y − 3x < 6}": "Step 1: The boundary line passes through (0, 6) and (2, 0): gradient = (0 − 6)/(2 − 0) = −3, intercept 6, so y = −3x + 6, i.e. y + 3x = 6. Step 2: Test the origin, which lies in the shaded region: 0 + 0 = 0 < 6. Step 3: So the shaded region is y + 3x < 6: B. The trap: y − 3x < 6 (D) uses gradient +3, a line rising to the right. Memory tip: find the line from its intercepts, then test a point such as (0, 0) to choose the inequality sign.",
 "|If the operation * in the set of integers is defined by p * q = √(pq), find the value of 4 * (8 * 32).|8|3|16|4": "Step 1: Work the bracket first: 8 * 32 = √(8 × 32) = √256 = 16. Step 2: Now 4 * 16 = √(4 × 16) = √64 = 8: A. The trap: stopping after the bracket gives 16 (C); working (4 * 8) first ignores the bracket, which says 8 * 32 comes first. Memory tip: for binary operations, always evaluate the innermost bracket first and substitute into the rule exactly.",
 "|Find the remainder when 3x³ + 5x² − 11x + 4 is divided by x + 3.|−4|4|1|−1": "Step 1: Remainder theorem: dividing f(x) by (x + 3) leaves remainder f(−3). Step 2: f(−3) = 3(−27) + 5(9) − 11(−3) + 4 = −81 + 45 + 33 + 4. Step 3: −81 + 82 = 1: C. The trap: substituting x = +3 instead of −3 gives 81 + 45 − 33 + 4 = 97; a sign slip on −11(−3) gives −65. Memory tip: divisor (x − a) → remainder f(a); for (x + 3), a = −3.",
 "|The nth terms of two sequences are Q_{n} = 3 · 2^{n−2} and U_{m} = 3 · 2^{2m−3}. Find the product of Q_{2} and U_{2}.|18|12|6|3": "Step 1: Q₂: put n = 2 into 3 · 2^{n−2}: 3 · 2^{0} = 3 × 1 = 3. Step 2: U₂: put m = 2 into 3 · 2^{2m−3}: 3 · 2^{1} = 6. Step 3: Product = 3 × 6 = 18: A. The trap: reading \"3.2\" as the decimal 3.2 gives very different values; here it means 3 × 2. Memory tip: any non-zero number to the power 0 is 1; substitute carefully into the exponent first.",
 "|If P = [[0, 0, −1], [3, 4, 5], [−1, 0, 1]], then |P| is|4|8|−8|0": "Step 1: Expand along the top row, which has two zeros: |P| = 0 − 0 + (−1) × det[[3, 4], [−1, 0]]. Step 2: det[[3, 4], [−1, 0]] = 3 × 0 − 4 × (−1) = 4. Step 3: |P| = −1 × 4 = −4. That is not among the options. The key gives D (0), which is the determinant when the top-left entry is 1 (a likely printing slip): 1 × 4 − 0 + (−1) × 4 = 0. The trap: dropping a sign in the 2 × 2 minor gives 4 (A). Memory tip: expand along the row with the most zeros, using the + − + sign pattern.",
 "|Factorize completely ac − 2bc − a² + 4b².|(a − 2b)(c − a − 2b)|(a − 2b)(c + a + 2b)|(a − 2b)(c + a + 2b)|(a − 2b)(c + a − 2b)": "Step 1: Group in pairs: (ac − 2bc) − (a² − 4b²). Step 2: Factorise each: c(a − 2b) − (a − 2b)(a + 2b), using the difference of two squares. Step 3: Take out the common factor (a − 2b): (a − 2b)(c − a − 2b): A. The trap: forgetting that the minus in front of the bracket applies to the whole of (a + 2b) gives (c + a + 2b) (B or C). Memory tip: a² − 4b² = (a − 2b)(a + 2b); look for a common bracket after grouping.",
 "|Find the sum to infinity of the series ½, 1/6, 1/18, …|2/3|1/3|3/4|1": "Step 1: The paper prints the series as ½, 1/6, 1/8, …, but that is not geometric; the third term is a misprint for 1/18. Step 2: a = ½, r = (1/6) ÷ (½) = 1/3, and (1/18) ÷ (1/6) = 1/3 confirms it. Step 3: S∞ = a/(1 − r) = ½ ÷ ⅔ = 3/4: C, the key's answer. The trap: using r = 3 (dividing the wrong way) breaks the formula, since |r| must be less than 1. Memory tip: S∞ = a/(1 − r), valid only when −1 < r < 1.",
 "|The length L of a simple pendulum varies directly as the square root of its period T. If a pendulum with period 4 sec is 64 cm long, find the length of a pendulum whose period is 9 seconds.|96 cm|324 cm|36 cm|324 cm": "Step 1: L varies directly as √T: L = k√T. Step 2: 64 = k√4 = 2k, so k = 32. Step 3: T = 9: L = 32 × √9 = 32 × 3 = 96 cm: A. The trap: using L ∝ T gives 64 × 9/4 = 144, and squaring instead of square-rooting gives 64 × 81/16 = 324 cm (B and D). Memory tip: write the variation as an equation with a constant k, find k, then substitute.",
 "|Find x and y respectively in the subtraction 4243 − 13x4 = y344, carried out in base 5 (x and y are single digits).|4, 2|2, 4|4, 3|3, 2": "Step 1: Units (base 5): 3 − 4 needs a borrow: 3 + 5 − 4 = 4 ✓. Step 2: Fives: 4 − 1 (borrowed) = 3; 3 − x must end in 4, so borrow: 3 + 5 − x = 4, x = 4. Step 3: Twenty-fives: 2 − 1 = 1; 1 − 3 needs a borrow: 6 − 3 = 3 ✓. Step 4: Last column: 4 − 1 − 1 = 2, so y = 2. Answer x = 4, y = 2: A. Check: 4243₅ = 573, 1344₅ = 224, 2344₅ = 349 = 573 − 224. The trap: borrowing 10 instead of 5. Memory tip: in base b, a borrow adds b to the column.",
 "|Three teachers shared a packet of chalk. The first teacher got 2/5 of the chalk and the second teacher received 2/15 of the remainder. What fraction did the third teacher receive?|8/15|11/25|12/25|13/25": "Step 1: First teacher: 2/5, so the remainder is 1 − 2/5 = 3/5. Step 2: Second teacher: 2/15 of the remainder = 2/15 × 3/5 = 6/75 = 2/25. Step 3: Third teacher: 3/5 − 2/25 = 15/25 − 2/25 = 13/25: D. The trap: taking 2/15 of the whole packet gives 1 − 2/5 − 2/15 = 7/15, which is not offered. Memory tip: \"of the remainder\" means multiply by the remainder first.",
 "|If 6 log_{x} 2 − 3 log_{x} 3 = 3 log_{5} 0.2, find x.|8/3|4/3|3/4|3/8": "Step 1: Right side: 0.2 = 1/5 = 5^{−1}, so log_{5} 0.2 = −1 and 3 log_{5} 0.2 = −3. Step 2: Left side: 6 log_{x} 2 − 3 log_{x} 3 = log_{x} 2^{6} − log_{x} 3^{3} = log_{x} (64/27). Step 3: log_{x} (64/27) = −3 means x^{−3} = 64/27, so x³ = 27/64 and x = 3/4: C. The trap: dropping the minus gives x³ = 64/27 and x = 4/3 (B). Memory tip: n log a = log aⁿ; log_{x} N = k means x^{k} = N.",
 "|Find p, if 451_{6} − p_{7} = 305_{6}.|62_{7}|116_{7}|611_{7}|142_{7}": "Step 1: Convert to base ten: 451₆ = 4 × 36 + 5 × 6 + 1 = 175; 305₆ = 3 × 36 + 0 + 5 = 113. Step 2: p = 175 − 113 = 62 (base ten). Step 3: Convert 62 to base 7: 62 = 1 × 49 + 1 × 7 + 6, so p = 116₇: B. The trap: 62₇ (A) writes the base-ten answer with a base-7 label; in fact 62₇ = 44. Memory tip: convert everything to base ten, calculate, then convert to the base asked for.",
 "|In a class of 40 students, each student offers at least one of Physics and Chemistry. If the number of students that offer Physics is three times the number that offers both subjects and the number that offers Chemistry is twice the number that offers Physics, find the number of students that offer Physics only.|15|25|5|10": "Step 1: Let the number offering both be b. Physics = 3b; Chemistry = 2 × Physics = 6b. Step 2: Everyone takes at least one subject: n(P ∪ C) = 3b + 6b − b = 8b = 40, so b = 5. Step 3: Physics = 15; Physics only = 15 − 5 = 10: D. The trap: giving the full Physics total, 15 (A), forgets to remove those who also take Chemistry. Memory tip: n(A ∪ B) = n(A) + n(B) − n(A ∩ B); \"only A\" = n(A) − n(A ∩ B).",
 "|Given that ∛(4^{2x}) = 16, find the value of x.|4|6|3|2": "Step 1: Write the cube root as a power: ∛(4^{2x}) = 4^{2x/3}. Step 2: Write 16 as a power of 4: 16 = 4². Step 3: Equate exponents: 2x/3 = 2, so x = 3: C. Check: 4^{6} = 4096 and ∛4096 = 16. The trap: forgetting the cube root gives 2x = 2, x = 1, and treating it as a square root gives x = 2 (D). Memory tip: ⁿ√(a^{m}) = a^{m/n}; express both sides with the same base and equate powers.",
 "|Evaluate (1/10 × 2/3 + 1/4)/(1/2 ÷ 3/5 − 1/4).|7/12|19/35|2/25|19/60": "Step 1: Numerator: 1/10 × 2/3 = 2/30 = 1/15; 1/15 + 1/4 = 4/60 + 15/60 = 19/60. Step 2: Denominator: 1/2 ÷ 3/5 = 1/2 × 5/3 = 5/6; 5/6 − 1/4 = 10/12 − 3/12 = 7/12. Step 3: (19/60) ÷ (7/12) = 19/60 × 12/7 = 19/35: B. The trap: stopping at the numerator gives 19/60 (D); stopping at the denominator gives 7/12 (A). Memory tip: simplify the top and bottom separately (× and ÷ before + and −), then divide.",
 "|The shaded region in the Venn diagram is|P^{c} ∪ (Q ∩ R)|P^{c} ∩ (Q ∪ R)|P ∩ Q|P^{c} ∩ (Q ∩ R)": "Step 1: The shaded region lies inside circle Q and inside circle R, so it is in Q ∩ R. Step 2: It lies outside circle P, so it is also in P^{c}. Step 3: Combine with \"and\" (∩): P^{c} ∩ (Q ∩ R): D. The trap: P^{c} ∩ (Q ∪ R) (B) would also shade the parts of Q only and R only; using ∪ in A shades far too much. Memory tip: \"inside and outside\" conditions join with ∩; read each circle in turn: in or out?",
 "|Simplify 1/(√3 + 2) in the form a + b√3.|2 − √3|−2 − √3|2 + √3|−2 + √3": "Step 1: Rationalise: multiply top and bottom by the conjugate 2 − √3. Step 2: Denominator: (2 + √3)(2 − √3) = 4 − 3 = 1. Step 3: So 1/(√3 + 2) = 2 − √3: A. Check: 2 − √3 ≈ 0.268 and 1/3.732 ≈ 0.268. The trap: using √3 − 2 as the multiplier gives −2 + √3 (D), a sign error, since then the denominator is −1. Memory tip: (a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b removes the surd.",
 "|A farmer planted 5,000 grains of maize and harvested 5,000 cobs, each bearing 500 grains. What is the ratio of the number of grains sowed to the number harvested?|1 : 250,000|1 : 25,000|1 : 500|1 : 5,000": "Step 1: Grains harvested = 5,000 cobs × 500 grains = 2,500,000. Step 2: Ratio sown : harvested = 5,000 : 2,500,000. Step 3: Divide both by 5,000: 1 : 500: C. The trap: comparing grains sown with cobs gives 1 : 1, and miscounting the zeros in 2,500,000 ÷ 5,000 leads to 1 : 5,000 (D) or 1 : 25,000 (B). Memory tip: put both quantities in the same unit (here grains) before simplifying a ratio."
};
